WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.102
Narrator: These moldy car floors,

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:03.937
seats caked with debris,

00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:05.672
and crusty cup holders

00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:07.407
are in need of a proper clean.

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:12.645
We asked detailers to
share the filthiest cars

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:14.514
they have ever worked on.

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:16.683
They show us how a simple deep clean

00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:19.986
can transform even the
most forgotten vehicles.

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:23.490
To kick us off,

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:27.427
we'll follow Elizabeth
Renevier of Mo3 Detailing.

00:00:27.427 --> 00:00:29.863
She shares how to make
stained car interiors

00:00:29.863 --> 00:00:31.464
look brand new.

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:48.114
My name is Elizabeth Renevier.

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:51.684
I am the owner and
operator of Mo3 Detailing.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:20.447
Afterwards, I put on a pretreatment,

00:01:20.447 --> 00:01:23.450
and I let it sit for
about 10 to 15 minutes.

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:32.292
And then I agitate with a drill brush

00:01:32.292 --> 00:01:34.294
or a hard-bristle brush.

00:02:42.362 --> 00:02:45.165
So, for the floor mats,
I like to hang them up

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:47.433
and rinse them out really well,

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:50.837
because those can just be
filled with sand and dirt.

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:53.473
All that really gets deep into the fibers,

00:02:53.473 --> 00:02:55.875
and just vacuuming isn't enough.

00:03:32.078 --> 00:03:35.381
Narrator: Next up, Brent
Dicesare from WD Detailing

00:03:35.582 --> 00:03:38.518
cleans the smelliest car
he has ever worked on.

00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:40.320
My name is Brent Dicesare.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.188
I'm one half of WD Detailing.

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:43.690
And today, we're going to be cleaning

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:46.025
an extremely dirty car that used to belong

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:48.261
to a hoarder who's now in treatment.

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:51.664
When we first got this car,

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:54.200
we actually had to leave
it outside of our shop

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.769
for over a week just to let it air out,

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:59.672
because it was one of the
worst-smelling vehicles

00:03:59.672 --> 00:04:01.641
that we've ever been brought,

00:04:01.641 --> 00:04:04.877
only because there was
so much grime and grease

00:04:04.877 --> 00:04:07.480
in both the carpets and the seats

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.617
and there was just a ton
of garbage everywhere.

00:04:10.617 --> 00:04:13.686
When we opened the
doors, I started gagging.

00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:15.121
We both had respirators on.

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:16.689
We couldn't do it without it.

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:18.992
The first thing we did
was use the ozone machine.

00:04:18.992 --> 00:04:22.495
The ozone machine removes
air pollutants and odors

00:04:22.495 --> 00:04:23.963
while also killing bacteria

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:25.832
and viruses inside the car,

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:28.401
which makes it a lot safer
for us to work in there.

00:04:28.401 --> 00:04:29.102
After taking the seats out,

00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:31.404
the first thing that we do
is take all the trash out

00:04:31.404 --> 00:04:33.006
and completely vacuum the car.

00:04:33.006 --> 00:04:36.075
That way we can really see
what we're working with.

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:38.044
So, when you see a car in this condition,

00:04:38.044 --> 00:04:40.847
it doesn't always make
too much sense to save it,

00:04:40.847 --> 00:04:42.348
but this car was brought to us

00:04:42.348 --> 00:04:45.585
by a really, really nice
gentleman named Parker.

00:04:45.585 --> 00:04:47.020
He reached out to us and told us

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:48.721
that he wanted to clean the car

00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:51.157
and give it away to someone
who really needed it.

00:04:51.157 --> 00:04:52.992
That really touched us,
so we were instantly in.

00:04:54.661 --> 00:04:58.064
As we were vacuuming, we
found a pile of dead bugs

00:04:58.064 --> 00:04:59.732
that were just sitting on the carpet

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:01.901
right next to the back seat,

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:05.972
and it was mixed in
with some hair and just

00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:10.043
some of the goodies when
you're detailing disaster cars.

00:05:10.043 --> 00:05:13.313
When people leave food
or anything of that sort

00:05:13.313 --> 00:05:15.014
that starts breaking down,

00:05:15.014 --> 00:05:17.850
that's when the maggots start
appearing in their cars.

00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:19.686
They vacuum up pretty easily,

00:05:19.686 --> 00:05:22.755
as long as they don't
splatter all over the carpet.

00:05:22.755 --> 00:05:25.258
But even then, you can just extract them.

00:05:25.258 --> 00:05:27.193
This garbage was so stuck on,

00:05:27.193 --> 00:05:29.595
we literally could not
get it off with our hands.

00:05:29.595 --> 00:05:32.565
Even a power washer isn't
good enough to get it off.

00:05:32.565 --> 00:05:34.834
So we'll use a flathead
screwdriver occasionally.

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:37.737
That helps get stuff off of the surface

00:05:37.737 --> 00:05:39.972
that we otherwise wouldn't be able to.

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:44.644
It actually wasn't sticky or oily.

00:05:44.644 --> 00:05:49.015
It was kind of like a
rough feeling, like Velcro.

00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:51.117
It's almost like that rough feeling.

00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:54.487
That's how bad this carpet was.

00:05:54.487 --> 00:05:56.322
If we didn't pull the
carpet out of this car,

00:05:56.322 --> 00:05:57.757
there was no possible way

00:05:57.757 --> 00:05:59.525
we were going to get it completely clean,

00:05:59.525 --> 00:06:01.561
especially with all the stains on it.

00:06:01.561 --> 00:06:03.830
You would normally want
to replace this carpet,

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:05.231
but we were trying to keep the budget

00:06:05.231 --> 00:06:07.233
as low as possible, as per Parker.

00:06:07.233 --> 00:06:09.168
That's what he wanted to do.

00:06:09.168 --> 00:06:11.938
So the original color of this car's

00:06:11.938 --> 00:06:15.441
carpets, floor mats, seats was a gray.

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:18.711
And when we got it,
they were almost black.

00:06:21.447 --> 00:06:23.916
The stains in the carpet came from ...

00:06:23.916 --> 00:06:25.251
I don't even know, honestly.

00:06:25.251 --> 00:06:27.153
We were just assuming that it's, like,

00:06:27.153 --> 00:06:29.655
grease and dirt and
everything mixed together.

00:06:36.262 --> 00:06:37.964
We have this carpet brush

00:06:37.964 --> 00:06:40.299
that will get any hairs out of the carpet,

00:06:40.299 --> 00:06:41.834
because this carpet also had

00:06:41.834 --> 00:06:44.103
what seemed like dog hair in it as well,

00:06:44.103 --> 00:06:48.875
and we got all that out
with a vacuum and our brush.

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:57.116
We used a degreaser instead
of an extractor soap,

00:06:57.116 --> 00:07:00.219
only because degreaser is a bit stronger.

00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:03.456
And then we went ahead
and drill-brushed it in

00:07:03.456 --> 00:07:05.057
to start getting it to work into

00:07:05.057 --> 00:07:06.626
the fibers of the carpet.

00:07:08.161 --> 00:07:09.762
We ended up having to power wash

00:07:09.762 --> 00:07:12.665
and extract this carpet four times.

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:18.571
After you spend 20
minutes on the same stain

00:07:18.571 --> 00:07:20.807
or half an hour and keep working at it

00:07:20.807 --> 00:07:24.310
and you can tell that
it's not changing color,

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:25.678
it's not coming out anymore,

00:07:25.678 --> 00:07:28.047
and there's really not
that much more you can do,

00:07:28.047 --> 00:07:31.083
you know as a detailer it's
kind of time to just call it.

00:07:33.152 --> 00:07:36.422
I would say 85% of the stains

00:07:36.422 --> 00:07:38.758
and the dirt were out of the carpet

00:07:38.758 --> 00:07:42.962
and about 15% that was
just complete damage

00:07:42.962 --> 00:07:44.630
was never going to come out.

00:07:47.867 --> 00:07:50.403
We hung it up inside of a closet

00:07:50.403 --> 00:07:51.504
where there's a heater.

00:07:51.504 --> 00:07:54.040
So we actually had to
let it dry for two days.

00:07:56.676 --> 00:07:57.777
So, dyeing the carpet

00:07:57.777 --> 00:07:59.879
was actually a last-minute decision

00:07:59.879 --> 00:08:01.647
that I ended up making myself.

00:08:01.647 --> 00:08:04.617
The first step is to
spray dye on the carpet.

00:08:04.617 --> 00:08:07.820
We use a brush to brush it into the carpet

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:10.389
to make sure it doesn't just sit,

00:08:10.389 --> 00:08:11.757
'cause if it sits, then the carpet

00:08:11.757 --> 00:08:15.228
will become, like, hard, bristly texture.

00:08:15.228 --> 00:08:16.529
And then you'll go back

00:08:16.529 --> 00:08:18.197
and you'll spray over it again.

00:08:18.197 --> 00:08:20.266
And then you just let it sit and dry,

00:08:20.266 --> 00:08:21.234
and that's really it.

00:08:21.234 --> 00:08:22.502
The only things you
want to be careful about

00:08:22.502 --> 00:08:25.137
are inhaling the fumes, so
we wore our respirators.

00:08:25.137 --> 00:08:26.606
And because we're in a parking garage,

00:08:26.606 --> 00:08:29.175
we have a really big exhaust
fan right next to us,

00:08:29.175 --> 00:08:31.110
and we had that running the entire time.

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:33.913
And we don't do this on normal carpets,

00:08:33.913 --> 00:08:36.349
or, honestly, ever,

00:08:36.349 --> 00:08:38.618
but when we saw that some of the stains

00:08:38.618 --> 00:08:40.219
weren't coming out, we wanted to make sure

00:08:40.219 --> 00:08:42.522
that this is going to
really change someone's life

00:08:42.522 --> 00:08:45.324
and they're going to be
happy when they receive it.

00:08:46.225 --> 00:08:48.127
Embedded in the seats are grease.

00:08:48.127 --> 00:08:49.829
And there was so much of it,

00:08:49.829 --> 00:08:52.465
after we did the entire extraction

00:08:52.465 --> 00:08:54.634
the first time, we let it dry overnight,

00:08:54.634 --> 00:08:56.435
and we came back the next day,

00:08:56.435 --> 00:08:59.572
and it was completely
covered in grease again.

00:08:59.572 --> 00:09:02.775
So everything that was farther in the seat

00:09:02.775 --> 00:09:03.910
came to the surface.

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:06.212
So we had to clean them a second time.

00:09:06.212 --> 00:09:08.614
So, after we spray our extractor soap,

00:09:08.614 --> 00:09:11.117
we'll let the soap sit
for about a minute or two,

00:09:11.117 --> 00:09:12.585
and then we'll go ahead
and use the drill brush

00:09:12.585 --> 00:09:15.488
to agitate the soap into the fibers,

00:09:15.488 --> 00:09:18.224
and then we'll clean it
out with the extractor.

00:09:18.224 --> 00:09:20.026
The extractor has a little nozzle

00:09:20.026 --> 00:09:22.962
that sprays hot water into
the fibers of the seat.

00:09:22.962 --> 00:09:24.997
And then it also has a vacuum head.

00:09:24.997 --> 00:09:27.900
So as it's spraying the water into it,

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:30.570
it's sucking the water
out at the same time.

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:32.405
That way it can clean it.

00:09:32.405 --> 00:09:33.739
We have a clear head on it,

00:09:33.739 --> 00:09:35.875
so we can actually see all of the soap,

00:09:35.875 --> 00:09:38.110
all of the dirt coming out of the seat.

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:41.013
And when that water coming
through is completely clear,

00:09:41.013 --> 00:09:43.149
that's how you know
when the seat is clean.

00:09:46.619 --> 00:09:48.754
So, now we're cleaning
the back seat of the car,

00:09:48.754 --> 00:09:51.691
which was also one of the
grossest parts of the car.

00:09:51.691 --> 00:09:53.759
And the first thing we
did was use the Tornador

00:09:53.759 --> 00:09:56.062
to spray off any of the dirt.

00:09:56.062 --> 00:09:58.798
And that is simply just
an air-compressor tool

00:09:58.798 --> 00:10:02.401
that shoots out air in
a tornado formation.

00:10:02.401 --> 00:10:04.370
And that'll get a lot of the dirt

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:06.472
out of the deeper fibers of the seat.

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:08.975
And then after that,

00:10:08.975 --> 00:10:11.410
we'll go on to the extracting process.

00:10:26.959 --> 00:10:28.327
The Tornador's best for getting

00:10:28.327 --> 00:10:30.096
any kind of microparticles

00:10:30.096 --> 00:10:32.264
out of the carpet or out of the seats

00:10:32.264 --> 00:10:35.067
and then dust or dirt

00:10:35.067 --> 00:10:37.870
or anything that's on the
topical layer as well.

00:10:38.371 --> 00:10:40.306
So, at this point, we're
taking apart the center console

00:10:40.306 --> 00:10:41.807
so we can get it out of the car

00:10:41.807 --> 00:10:44.443
and pressure wash all
of the disgusting grease

00:10:44.443 --> 00:10:46.612
and mold and everything off of it.

00:10:46.612 --> 00:10:50.082
Dirt definitely gets behind both the radio

00:10:50.082 --> 00:10:52.385
or any of the knobs on the center console,

00:10:52.385 --> 00:10:54.720
because that's where your
hands are touching a lot.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:57.089
So by dissembling those things

00:10:57.089 --> 00:10:58.290
and cleaning behind them,

00:10:58.290 --> 00:10:59.992
we know that we're sanitizing

00:10:59.992 --> 00:11:02.828
and doing the best job
that we possibly can.

00:11:03.663 --> 00:11:05.665
Sometimes the Tornador can't get things

00:11:05.665 --> 00:11:07.233
that are super stuck on

00:11:07.233 --> 00:11:10.169
if they're gooey or anything like that,

00:11:10.169 --> 00:11:13.372
almost like a glue substance to plastics.

00:11:13.372 --> 00:11:15.241
The air is not going to
do a good enough job,

00:11:15.241 --> 00:11:18.444
so you have to scrape it
off and power wash it.

00:11:26.919 --> 00:11:29.422
We wanted to give it a good
exterior cleaning, too.

00:11:29.422 --> 00:11:31.791
It did have a thick layer
of dust on the exterior.

00:11:31.791 --> 00:11:33.192
The engine bay was dirty.

00:11:33.192 --> 00:11:35.494
The wheels had brake dust all over them.

00:11:38.464 --> 00:11:39.865
Surprisingly enough,

00:11:39.865 --> 00:11:43.803
dirt or dust actually acts
as a protectant to paint.

00:11:43.803 --> 00:11:48.007
So when a car is dusty or
covered in a layer of dirt,

00:11:48.007 --> 00:11:50.342
it protects it from the sun's UV rays.

00:11:53.612 --> 00:11:54.980
So, the first thing that we do is

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:57.083
we spray our degreaser all over the paint,

00:11:57.083 --> 00:11:59.518
'cause it's paint-safe,
and that'll just start

00:11:59.518 --> 00:12:02.288
loosening up any of the
dirt on the outside.

00:12:02.288 --> 00:12:04.056
And then we'll use our power washer

00:12:04.056 --> 00:12:05.324
to get the dirt off.

00:12:31.751 --> 00:12:33.085
So, after we wash the car,

00:12:33.085 --> 00:12:35.154
we go ahead and we spray
the entire thing down

00:12:35.154 --> 00:12:37.957
with the ceramic sealant
while it's still wet.

00:12:37.957 --> 00:12:39.959
And that just gives it a
little bit of protection

00:12:39.959 --> 00:12:42.962
and also a shine after we wash it.

00:12:42.962 --> 00:12:45.030
And then we'll go ahead
and spray it down again

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:47.099
before drying it.

00:12:48.667 --> 00:12:50.069
After we were done with it,

00:12:50.069 --> 00:12:52.872
he actually needed to take
it back to his home shop

00:12:52.872 --> 00:12:54.540
and do some mechanical work on it

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:56.242
to make sure it's safe for the people,

00:12:56.242 --> 00:12:57.743
whoever owns it next.

00:12:57.743 --> 00:12:59.645
We're really happy with
the result of this car,

00:12:59.645 --> 00:13:03.015
but what meant the most to
us is how Parker reacted,

00:13:03.015 --> 00:13:05.451
because this was something special to him.

00:13:05.451 --> 00:13:07.686
And the fact that we
got to be a part of it

00:13:07.686 --> 00:13:09.255
is why we did it in the first place.

00:13:10.956 --> 00:13:12.892
We do all these details
absolutely for free.

00:13:12.892 --> 00:13:15.761
So we took this on because
we wanted to make a change

00:13:15.761 --> 00:13:17.997
and we wanted to help a
family that needed it.

00:13:28.574 --> 00:13:31.811
Narrator: For this car, Brent
tackles months of mold growth

00:13:31.811 --> 00:13:33.813
caused by water damage.

00:13:35.981 --> 00:13:37.216
My name's Brent Dicesare,

00:13:37.216 --> 00:13:39.084
and I'm half of WD Detailing.

00:13:39.084 --> 00:13:40.386
And today we're going to be cleaning

00:13:40.386 --> 00:13:41.754
a super-moldy car

00:13:41.754 --> 00:13:44.890
that has grass growing in it, mold, water,

00:13:44.890 --> 00:13:46.058
and all of the above.

00:13:46.058 --> 00:13:47.593
It was absolutely disgusting.

00:13:51.063 --> 00:13:53.566
First, we put the ozone
machine inside the car.

00:13:53.566 --> 00:13:55.401
It basically pumps oxygen

00:13:55.401 --> 00:13:57.269
to the third degree into it,

00:13:57.269 --> 00:13:58.437
or ozone,

00:13:58.437 --> 00:14:01.574
and it suffocates the
bacteria and kills it.

00:14:01.574 --> 00:14:02.875
And then we'll pull the seats out

00:14:02.875 --> 00:14:04.443
after we do the ozone treatment,

00:14:04.443 --> 00:14:05.477
so we can actually

00:14:05.477 --> 00:14:08.113
get to all of the spots that we need to.

00:14:08.113 --> 00:14:10.516
And then the first thing
that we'll do is vacuum it,

00:14:10.516 --> 00:14:12.418
try to get all the
debris, or, in this case,

00:14:12.418 --> 00:14:13.786
the mold and the water out of it

00:14:13.786 --> 00:14:16.655
so we can really get in
the car and work on it.

00:14:16.655 --> 00:14:18.390
So, here we're working with

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:20.392
where all of the water ended up going.

00:14:20.392 --> 00:14:22.828
The water was only on half of the car,

00:14:22.828 --> 00:14:25.598
and I think that's because of
where the leak was located.

00:14:25.598 --> 00:14:27.233
So what you're looking at right now

00:14:27.233 --> 00:14:30.135
is us vacuuming up all of that water.

00:14:32.805 --> 00:14:36.242
The last time that this
car was plated was in 2015,

00:14:36.242 --> 00:14:39.178
so it ended up sitting
for the last six years.

00:14:39.178 --> 00:14:40.880
And just about five months ago,

00:14:40.880 --> 00:14:42.348
the owner noticed that there was

00:14:42.348 --> 00:14:43.549
an issue with the sunroof

00:14:43.549 --> 00:14:45.084
where it wouldn't close all the way

00:14:45.084 --> 00:14:47.753
and water was continuously
getting in there,

00:14:47.753 --> 00:14:50.089
but we've never seen
anything like this before.

00:14:54.026 --> 00:14:55.494
For a car like this,

00:14:55.494 --> 00:14:57.763
it's actually recommended
to remove the carpet,

00:14:57.763 --> 00:14:59.765
throw it away, and replace it.

00:14:59.765 --> 00:15:01.867
But for some reason, in this specific car,

00:15:01.867 --> 00:15:03.535
it was stapled down completely

00:15:03.535 --> 00:15:05.804
to the point where if we
were going to remove it,

00:15:05.804 --> 00:15:07.106
it wasn't going to go back in

00:15:07.106 --> 00:15:09.041
and sit the way that it would need to.

00:15:09.041 --> 00:15:09.975
For this car,

00:15:09.975 --> 00:15:12.344
it was definitely a different process

00:15:12.344 --> 00:15:14.513
than when we would normally extract a car,

00:15:14.513 --> 00:15:16.982
because the carpet was basically stained

00:15:16.982 --> 00:15:18.183
with that green mold.

00:15:18.183 --> 00:15:20.185
And we had to try to figure out a way

00:15:20.185 --> 00:15:22.354
to push it out of the fibers of the carpet

00:15:22.354 --> 00:15:24.056
and see if the carpet would revive

00:15:24.056 --> 00:15:26.292
to the white color that it ended up being.

00:15:26.292 --> 00:15:27.593
So the first thing that we did,

00:15:27.593 --> 00:15:29.028
which we normally would not do,

00:15:29.028 --> 00:15:30.496
is use the Tornador attachment,

00:15:30.496 --> 00:15:33.465
which is compressed air
that shoots out in a circle,

00:15:33.465 --> 00:15:34.900
like a tornado almost.

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:36.335
You have a little compartment

00:15:36.335 --> 00:15:37.903
where you put all-purpose cleaner in

00:15:37.903 --> 00:15:39.838
mixed with water, so you dilute it,

00:15:39.838 --> 00:15:43.042
and you attach it to an air
hose on an air compressor.

00:15:43.042 --> 00:15:45.911
And it really gets into
the fibers of that cloth

00:15:45.911 --> 00:15:48.981
and kind of blows out anything
that might be in there.

00:15:48.981 --> 00:15:51.717
So we blew out the entire carpet with it

00:15:51.717 --> 00:15:53.385
after vacuuming it.

00:15:53.385 --> 00:15:54.653
And then we'll go ahead

00:15:54.653 --> 00:15:56.889
and we'll use our extractor soap on it

00:15:56.889 --> 00:15:59.291
and spray a healthy amount on there.

00:15:59.291 --> 00:16:01.360
And we'll actually use the extractor,

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:04.363
which shoots hot water into the carpet,

00:16:04.363 --> 00:16:07.299
to get the carpet a little bit more wet,

00:16:07.299 --> 00:16:09.068
because when we use our drill brush,

00:16:09.068 --> 00:16:11.236
we want to try to reduce
the amount of friction

00:16:11.236 --> 00:16:13.272
that we're putting into the carpet.

00:16:13.272 --> 00:16:14.773
Because if there's too much friction,

00:16:14.773 --> 00:16:16.442
if it's not wet enough,

00:16:16.442 --> 00:16:19.044
then it can actually damage the carpet.

00:16:19.044 --> 00:16:22.614
We'll use our drill brush
to agitate all of that soap.

00:16:22.614 --> 00:16:24.450
So we'll go over it a few times,

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:26.418
get it really into the fibers.

00:16:26.418 --> 00:16:28.387
And then we'll use our
hot-water extractor,

00:16:28.387 --> 00:16:30.389
which only has water in it.

00:16:30.389 --> 00:16:32.458
We don't ever recommend
putting any kind of soaps

00:16:32.458 --> 00:16:33.525
or anything in your extractor,

00:16:33.525 --> 00:16:36.161
because when you're blowing
the water into the carpet,

00:16:36.161 --> 00:16:37.596
you want to suck everything out,

00:16:37.596 --> 00:16:39.098
and if you have soap in it,

00:16:39.098 --> 00:16:40.299
it's just going to add more to it

00:16:40.299 --> 00:16:42.234
and it's not going to be completely clean.

00:16:44.203 --> 00:16:48.340
I actually filled half of
a Shop-Vac full of water.

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:49.875
So I think I actually got

00:16:49.875 --> 00:16:53.746
probably 95% to 99% of the water out.

00:16:55.481 --> 00:16:56.882
When you do the doors, it's the same thing

00:16:56.882 --> 00:16:58.984
as doing any of the other
plastics in the car.

00:16:58.984 --> 00:17:00.519
In this case, we used Triclean,

00:17:00.519 --> 00:17:02.454
which is made to kill bacteria

00:17:02.454 --> 00:17:04.323
and all that stuff inside of cars.

00:17:04.323 --> 00:17:07.559
While most all-purpose
cleaners do kill bacteria,

00:17:07.559 --> 00:17:10.396
we wanted something that
is a little bit stronger.

00:17:10.396 --> 00:17:12.097
Especially because in this case,

00:17:12.097 --> 00:17:13.132
there's a lot more mold

00:17:13.132 --> 00:17:16.035
than you would normally
see in a regular car.

00:17:16.035 --> 00:17:17.669
And then use the Tornador attachment

00:17:17.669 --> 00:17:19.638
or some soft-bristle brushes

00:17:19.638 --> 00:17:21.407
and really get in every nook and cranny

00:17:21.407 --> 00:17:22.975
and make sure we clean it up.

00:17:28.347 --> 00:17:29.248
So, for this part,

00:17:29.248 --> 00:17:30.916
he's actually drying off the door

00:17:30.916 --> 00:17:31.950
and getting all the liquid

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:32.851
out of the nooks and crannies.

00:17:32.851 --> 00:17:34.420
So that is the Tornador attachment,

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:35.554
but that's a different one

00:17:35.554 --> 00:17:38.057
than the one that is used
for specifically cleaning.

00:17:38.057 --> 00:17:40.225
So this Tornador attachment doesn't have

00:17:40.225 --> 00:17:42.261
the canister that holds
any kind of cleaner,

00:17:42.261 --> 00:17:44.063
and this one just blows out air.

00:17:44.063 --> 00:17:46.899
So you use that to get
into cracks and crevices

00:17:46.899 --> 00:17:48.434
where you normally couldn't with a brush

00:17:48.434 --> 00:17:50.369
or with a microfiber towel,

00:17:50.369 --> 00:17:53.605
or you use it to get under seats.

00:17:53.605 --> 00:17:54.740
When we do leather seats

00:17:54.740 --> 00:17:56.275
that have been sitting in mold,

00:17:56.275 --> 00:17:58.544
the first thing we do
with any seat is vacuum it

00:17:58.544 --> 00:18:00.012
just to get any kind of debris.

00:18:00.012 --> 00:18:01.346
Especially in the crevice

00:18:01.346 --> 00:18:04.083
where the bottom and the
back of the seat meet,

00:18:04.083 --> 00:18:05.451
we'll use an all-purpose cleaner

00:18:05.451 --> 00:18:06.819
or a leather cleaner.

00:18:06.819 --> 00:18:07.786
They're two different things.

00:18:07.786 --> 00:18:09.822
A leather cleaner is more suitable

00:18:09.822 --> 00:18:11.056
for high-end leather.

00:18:11.056 --> 00:18:13.058
And then he'll spray it on there,

00:18:13.058 --> 00:18:14.693
use a Colourlock brush.

00:18:14.693 --> 00:18:17.796
So it's a really fine, strong brush

00:18:17.796 --> 00:18:21.166
to get into all of the pores of the seat.

00:18:21.166 --> 00:18:22.267
When we're cleaning leather seats,

00:18:22.267 --> 00:18:23.769
we don't use too much pressure

00:18:23.769 --> 00:18:25.737
just because you don't
want to damage them.

00:18:25.737 --> 00:18:28.307
You could leave scratches,
you could tear them.

00:18:28.307 --> 00:18:32.144
Spray it again, use the
steamer to kill any bacteria,

00:18:32.144 --> 00:18:33.912
and then wipe that away too.

00:18:35.347 --> 00:18:36.515
When we do the wheels, the first thing

00:18:36.515 --> 00:18:38.417
we like to do is pressure wash them off

00:18:38.417 --> 00:18:39.852
and get any of that initial dirt

00:18:39.852 --> 00:18:41.420
or grime off of them.

00:18:41.420 --> 00:18:43.255
We have some heavy acid that we'll use

00:18:43.255 --> 00:18:44.123
if it's really bad,

00:18:44.123 --> 00:18:47.159
but we don't use it on painted rims.

00:18:47.159 --> 00:18:47.993
And then we'll go ahead

00:18:47.993 --> 00:18:49.461
and we'll agitate it with a brush,

00:18:49.461 --> 00:18:51.263
and we'll use a few different brushes

00:18:51.263 --> 00:18:54.633
to really get in both the wheel well,

00:18:54.633 --> 00:18:57.269
on the tire, inside the rim,

00:18:57.269 --> 00:18:59.204
and the drum of the rim.

00:18:59.204 --> 00:19:01.540
And then we'll go ahead
and pressure wash it away.

00:19:06.445 --> 00:19:07.446
So, for the exterior,

00:19:07.446 --> 00:19:09.348
because it had been
sitting out for so long,

00:19:09.348 --> 00:19:10.883
over time if you don't clean it,

00:19:10.883 --> 00:19:12.851
grime will get attached to the paint

00:19:12.851 --> 00:19:14.453
and start growing mold.

00:19:14.453 --> 00:19:16.688
Because we did the
exterior of the car first,

00:19:16.688 --> 00:19:18.624
we weren't too worried
about water getting in,

00:19:18.624 --> 00:19:21.059
since it was already full of water.

00:19:21.059 --> 00:19:21.894
So we just went ahead

00:19:21.894 --> 00:19:23.929
and we pressure washed everything out.

00:19:23.929 --> 00:19:26.598
We put paint-safe degreaser
all over the paint

00:19:26.598 --> 00:19:28.867
because it just had a really thick layer

00:19:28.867 --> 00:19:30.802
of both mold and grime on it

00:19:30.802 --> 00:19:31.870
that wouldn't just come off

00:19:31.870 --> 00:19:33.572
with an initial pressure wash.

00:19:33.572 --> 00:19:35.707
We let it sit for about
two to three minutes,

00:19:35.707 --> 00:19:37.609
and then we pressure washed it again.

00:19:37.609 --> 00:19:39.845
And then we'll go ahead and
we'll use the foam cannon.

00:19:39.845 --> 00:19:42.047
So, the foam is just a car soap,

00:19:42.047 --> 00:19:43.682
and you just put it into a foam cannon

00:19:43.682 --> 00:19:45.951
and you attach it to your pressure washer.

00:19:45.951 --> 00:19:48.954
It acts as a lubricant for
any dirt that's on the car.

00:19:48.954 --> 00:19:50.556
So when you go to wash it,

00:19:50.556 --> 00:19:51.623
you're really minimizing

00:19:51.623 --> 00:19:54.493
how much scratching can
happen to the paint.

00:19:54.493 --> 00:19:55.327
And then we'll go ahead

00:19:55.327 --> 00:19:56.962
and we'll use our microfiber mitts

00:19:56.962 --> 00:19:59.231
to wash it and then spray it off.

00:20:00.465 --> 00:20:02.534
The absolute best way to store a car

00:20:02.534 --> 00:20:03.669
if it's going to be outside

00:20:03.669 --> 00:20:06.205
is with some sort of car cover or a tarp.

00:20:06.205 --> 00:20:07.739
That way, no water is going to get

00:20:07.739 --> 00:20:08.674
on the top of the car

00:20:08.674 --> 00:20:10.375
and find its way into the car.

00:20:10.375 --> 00:20:11.877
It'll keep the paint clean

00:20:11.877 --> 00:20:13.478
and away from all the elements.

00:20:13.478 --> 00:20:15.447
If it's going to be a car
that you're not driving

00:20:15.447 --> 00:20:17.249
for extended periods of time,

00:20:17.249 --> 00:20:18.150
you still want to look in it

00:20:18.150 --> 00:20:20.185
and make sure nothing's going wrong,

00:20:20.185 --> 00:20:22.054
because seals can give.

00:20:22.054 --> 00:20:24.423
And if they do and
water starts getting in,

00:20:24.423 --> 00:20:26.058
then that's a whole world of problems.

00:20:26.058 --> 00:20:29.461
So the best way to stop
mold is to be proactive

00:20:29.461 --> 00:20:32.764
and check on your car before
it actually starts growing.

00:20:33.999 --> 00:20:35.400
When it comes to detailing,

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:38.270
I believe it's best to
maybe get a full detail

00:20:38.270 --> 00:20:40.606
once to twice a year, maybe three times.

00:20:40.606 --> 00:20:42.274
I always tell people to vacuum it,

00:20:42.274 --> 00:20:45.177
if not every few weeks,
at least once a month.

00:20:49.014 --> 00:20:51.583
Narrator: RJ Wagner from WD Detailing

00:20:51.583 --> 00:20:54.720
now attempts to revive
this unusually muddy car.

00:20:54.987 --> 00:20:57.589
I'm RJ. I'm one half of WD Detailing,

00:20:57.589 --> 00:20:59.758
and today we're going to
be showing you how we clean

00:20:59.758 --> 00:21:03.128
quite possibly the
muddiest WS6 Firebird ever.

00:21:03.128 --> 00:21:05.564
It was involved in a really
bad flood in Kentucky.

00:21:05.564 --> 00:21:07.499
The car was absolutely covered,

00:21:07.499 --> 00:21:09.968
basically head to toe, in caked-on mud,

00:21:09.968 --> 00:21:11.470
to the point to where the mud had dried

00:21:11.470 --> 00:21:12.671
and it was starting to crack,

00:21:12.671 --> 00:21:14.206
almost like a desert floor.

00:21:15.507 --> 00:21:16.341
Rock hard.

00:21:16.341 --> 00:21:18.677
I can only assume that it was involved

00:21:18.677 --> 00:21:20.712
in very fast-moving water,

00:21:20.712 --> 00:21:23.081
it picked up sediment
from somewhere nearby,

00:21:23.081 --> 00:21:24.483
and then it dried out.

00:21:26.652 --> 00:21:28.186
So, the first step is removing

00:21:28.186 --> 00:21:31.156
pretty much anything
except for the dashboard

00:21:31.156 --> 00:21:32.991
and the steering wheel
and the steering column,

00:21:32.991 --> 00:21:35.193
because that was a
little bit more involved

00:21:35.193 --> 00:21:36.862
than we really had time for.

00:21:39.431 --> 00:21:41.400
We're using the vacuum to remove

00:21:41.400 --> 00:21:44.303
any residual water in
the back of the trunk.

00:21:44.303 --> 00:21:45.437
Going into this detail,

00:21:45.437 --> 00:21:47.105
I knew a lot of this vehicle

00:21:47.105 --> 00:21:48.407
was going to be sold for parts.

00:21:48.407 --> 00:21:50.475
Because there was only X amount made

00:21:50.475 --> 00:21:53.045
and only a certain amount
of parts available,

00:21:53.045 --> 00:21:55.180
it wouldn't make sense to scrap that car,

00:21:55.180 --> 00:21:58.016
especially with so many
parts still being functional.

00:21:58.016 --> 00:21:59.217
It changed some of the ways

00:21:59.217 --> 00:22:00.719
that we were going to
be cleaning the vehicle

00:22:00.719 --> 00:22:01.753
and using some methods

00:22:01.753 --> 00:22:03.855
that we wouldn't use in a normal detail.

00:22:03.855 --> 00:22:05.123
And we've never done this before,

00:22:05.123 --> 00:22:07.426
but we literally took a pressure washer

00:22:07.426 --> 00:22:10.962
and sprayed out the whole
interior of the vehicle

00:22:10.962 --> 00:22:12.464
in order to remove all the mud.

00:22:12.464 --> 00:22:14.166
So, the reason why we wouldn't do that

00:22:14.166 --> 00:22:17.135
in a normal detail job is
it would definitely damage

00:22:17.135 --> 00:22:19.137
some of the electrical
components of the vehicle

00:22:19.137 --> 00:22:22.007
if you didn't give the
car ample time to dry.

00:22:22.007 --> 00:22:24.643
So if this was a normal
vehicle that we did that in,

00:22:24.643 --> 00:22:26.378
we would have to disconnect the battery

00:22:26.378 --> 00:22:28.146
and then make sure that it dried out

00:22:28.146 --> 00:22:30.682
for over five or six days.

00:22:32.217 --> 00:22:33.819
With a car in this condition,

00:22:33.819 --> 00:22:36.188
to clean the carpet was again different

00:22:36.188 --> 00:22:38.256
than most vehicles that
we see on a daily basis.

00:22:38.256 --> 00:22:40.759
So we probably spent about 45 minutes

00:22:40.759 --> 00:22:43.095
just pressure washing gallons of water

00:22:43.095 --> 00:22:44.830
into the carpet in order to try and flush

00:22:44.830 --> 00:22:47.065
as much mud out of it as possible.

00:22:48.667 --> 00:22:50.569
Well, not only that;
we had to flip it over

00:22:50.569 --> 00:22:53.472
and spray out mud on the other side.

00:22:53.472 --> 00:22:55.874
And then we actually had
to let it drain overnight

00:22:55.874 --> 00:22:57.609
to try and dry out as much as we could

00:22:57.609 --> 00:22:59.277
before we put it back in the next day

00:22:59.277 --> 00:23:01.813
to then continue to
clean it inside the car.

00:23:01.813 --> 00:23:03.148
So, once the carpet is dry,

00:23:03.148 --> 00:23:05.050
we then place it back into the vehicle

00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:06.918
and we use carpet soap.

00:23:06.918 --> 00:23:08.787
You spray it onto the carpet,

00:23:08.787 --> 00:23:10.689
and then I actually use the extractor

00:23:10.689 --> 00:23:12.124
to add more water

00:23:12.124 --> 00:23:13.992
in order to create a soapier solution,

00:23:13.992 --> 00:23:15.093
and then you agitate it

00:23:15.093 --> 00:23:18.063
using a brush that connects to a drill

00:23:18.063 --> 00:23:21.266
to release some of the dirt
and grime or the stains

00:23:21.266 --> 00:23:23.268
that are contained
within the carpet fibers,

00:23:23.268 --> 00:23:24.202
bring them to the top,

00:23:24.202 --> 00:23:27.205
so then you can suck it
away with the extractor.

00:23:28.240 --> 00:23:30.575
In the extractor we only use water.

00:23:30.575 --> 00:23:32.344
If you spray the soap onto the carpet

00:23:32.344 --> 00:23:34.980
and if you only have
water in your extractor,

00:23:34.980 --> 00:23:37.382
when you see through the
head of the extractor

00:23:37.382 --> 00:23:38.750
that there's no more foam,

00:23:38.750 --> 00:23:40.552
there's no more bubbles or soap

00:23:40.552 --> 00:23:41.887
that's coming out of the carpet,

00:23:41.887 --> 00:23:43.388
you know you've not only removed

00:23:43.388 --> 00:23:44.990
the contaminants that were in the carpet,

00:23:44.990 --> 00:23:48.326
but also any residue from leftover soap.

00:23:51.797 --> 00:23:53.465
When we were cleaning the leather,

00:23:53.465 --> 00:23:55.567
we had to pressure wash
the majority of the mud

00:23:55.567 --> 00:23:58.103
away first in order to
even reveal the leather

00:23:58.103 --> 00:24:01.106
to follow a normal type
of detailing method.

00:24:01.106 --> 00:24:03.074
There was too much mud on the surface

00:24:03.074 --> 00:24:05.076
to really remove it in any other way.

00:24:05.076 --> 00:24:07.846
So that's not typically how
we would clean leather seats,

00:24:07.846 --> 00:24:10.749
but drastic cases call
for drastic measures.

00:24:10.749 --> 00:24:14.252
We then used an all-purpose
cleaner in a Tornador.

00:24:14.252 --> 00:24:15.954
It's basically compressed air

00:24:15.954 --> 00:24:18.323
that feeds into a reservoir

00:24:18.323 --> 00:24:20.325
that holds an all-purpose cleaner,

00:24:20.325 --> 00:24:22.194
and then it uses that compressed air

00:24:22.194 --> 00:24:24.896
to pick up the solution and blast it into

00:24:24.896 --> 00:24:26.331
the material that you're trying to clean.

00:24:26.331 --> 00:24:27.766
We'll use it when we know that

00:24:27.766 --> 00:24:29.801
the leather has seen a lot of age

00:24:29.801 --> 00:24:32.604
and we don't want to
potentially damage it further.

00:24:32.604 --> 00:24:34.773
And then I went ahead
and used a leather brush

00:24:34.773 --> 00:24:36.975
in order to agitate that further

00:24:36.975 --> 00:24:40.912
and to remove as much mud
and sediment as possible.

00:24:40.912 --> 00:24:43.648
These would have to dry
for another couple days

00:24:43.648 --> 00:24:45.784
in a heated storage facility.

00:24:45.784 --> 00:24:47.285
With the exterior, we went ahead

00:24:47.285 --> 00:24:49.221
and just did a pretty basic wash.

00:24:49.221 --> 00:24:51.623
So we had to make sure that
we just blew out all the mud

00:24:51.623 --> 00:24:53.191
from all the cracks and crevices there.

00:24:53.191 --> 00:24:55.727
The foam cannon is my
personal favorite part

00:24:55.727 --> 00:24:58.163
when it comes to washing
the exterior of a vehicle.

00:24:58.163 --> 00:24:59.831
Foam and soap is the same thing.

00:24:59.831 --> 00:25:01.099
It's actually like shaving cream.

00:25:01.099 --> 00:25:02.300
You spray it onto your hand,

00:25:02.300 --> 00:25:04.636
and as you lather it up,
it becomes more foamy.

00:25:04.636 --> 00:25:06.505
It helps break down some of the grime,

00:25:06.505 --> 00:25:08.473
the grease, the sediment on the vehicle

00:25:08.473 --> 00:25:10.242
and then lifts it to the top,

00:25:10.242 --> 00:25:11.743
and that way as you're washing,

00:25:11.743 --> 00:25:14.479
there's both lubrication as
well as cleaning properties

00:25:14.479 --> 00:25:16.515
that help you remove the dirt and grime

00:25:16.515 --> 00:25:19.017
as efficiently and safely as possible.

00:25:19.017 --> 00:25:19.985
Cleaning the windows,

00:25:19.985 --> 00:25:22.153
most people ask what
solution you're using,

00:25:22.153 --> 00:25:23.722
what window cleaner are you using,

00:25:23.722 --> 00:25:24.990
but in reality,

00:25:24.990 --> 00:25:26.958
it's the type of towel that you use

00:25:26.958 --> 00:25:28.326
and how many towels you use

00:25:28.326 --> 00:25:29.427
and how clean they are.

00:25:29.427 --> 00:25:31.396
So, windows are very prone to streaking,

00:25:31.396 --> 00:25:33.331
especially when they've
been splattered with mud,

00:25:33.331 --> 00:25:35.400
like they were in this vehicle.

00:25:35.400 --> 00:25:38.103
So to start out, we just use
your standard shop towels

00:25:38.103 --> 00:25:39.604
in order to remove the bulk of the grime,

00:25:39.604 --> 00:25:41.740
and then we use another type of towel

00:25:41.740 --> 00:25:42.841
that's called GSM.

00:25:42.841 --> 00:25:45.410
So it has a higher grams per square meter

00:25:45.410 --> 00:25:48.046
of microfiber, and all that means is that

00:25:48.046 --> 00:25:49.915
there's more fibers on the towel,

00:25:49.915 --> 00:25:52.083
and the more grams per square meter,

00:25:52.083 --> 00:25:53.418
the fluffier the towel

00:25:53.418 --> 00:25:55.854
and the more microfiber strands there are

00:25:55.854 --> 00:25:58.189
to wipe away any of the streaks.

00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:03.061
They actually were able to get that car

00:26:03.061 --> 00:26:04.195
running again, though.

00:26:04.195 --> 00:26:06.898
It still functions, but
with a good conscious,

00:26:06.898 --> 00:26:09.200
they can't sell that car to another owner.

00:26:10.802 --> 00:26:13.405
When you finish it and you
look back at the result,

00:26:13.405 --> 00:26:15.407
we laugh because it's almost like,

00:26:15.407 --> 00:26:17.742
I don't know how it came out so well,

00:26:17.742 --> 00:26:19.377
but somehow it does.

00:26:29.020 --> 00:26:31.990
Narrator: This abandoned
car has layers of debris

00:26:31.990 --> 00:26:35.594
after sitting in a barn for 37 years.

00:26:40.532 --> 00:26:41.833
My name is Brent Dicesare.

00:26:41.833 --> 00:26:43.868
I'm one half of WD Detailing.

00:26:43.868 --> 00:26:45.370
And today we're going to be cleaning

00:26:45.370 --> 00:26:50.375
a 1978 Sterling Nova that
sat in a barn since 1985.

00:26:59.851 --> 00:27:02.153
So, a Sterling Nova is a kit car

00:27:02.153 --> 00:27:05.824
which is on a Volkswagen
Beetle chassis and engine.

00:27:05.824 --> 00:27:08.727
And it's really unique
because of the way it looks

00:27:08.727 --> 00:27:11.463
and the way that the top opens.

00:27:11.463 --> 00:27:13.398
There's no doors on this car.

00:27:13.398 --> 00:27:14.733
The way that you get in and out

00:27:14.733 --> 00:27:18.370
is literally by the whole top lifting up.

00:27:18.370 --> 00:27:19.704
We had to take the top off of this car

00:27:19.704 --> 00:27:22.173
because it actually couldn't
hold its own weight up

00:27:22.173 --> 00:27:25.777
and we didn't want to risk
it falling on one of us.

00:27:25.777 --> 00:27:28.480
As soon as we took the top off of the car,

00:27:28.480 --> 00:27:32.784
we found this mouse nest right
on the backside of the dash.

00:27:32.784 --> 00:27:36.054
It was made out of insulation or carpet

00:27:36.054 --> 00:27:37.789
or parts from the seats.

00:27:37.789 --> 00:27:41.393
Any random fibers that
they can find in the car

00:27:41.393 --> 00:27:43.361
they'll take and make a nest out of.

00:27:43.361 --> 00:27:45.163
And then we sanitized the area afterwards

00:27:45.163 --> 00:27:48.166
with TriClean, which is a cleaner

00:27:48.166 --> 00:27:50.168
that will kill all the bacteria.

00:27:50.168 --> 00:27:52.137
Cars attract mice because

00:27:52.137 --> 00:27:54.172
they know that it's a dark space,

00:27:54.172 --> 00:27:56.041
and when these cars sit in a barn

00:27:56.041 --> 00:28:00.278
and nobody's touching them
for 30-plus, 40-plus years,

00:28:00.278 --> 00:28:01.479
they're not going to be bothered,

00:28:01.479 --> 00:28:03.848
so they're going to
take advantage of that.

00:28:03.848 --> 00:28:04.783
And then after that

00:28:04.783 --> 00:28:06.051
we were able to take the seats out

00:28:06.051 --> 00:28:08.186
and really get in there
and clean everything.

00:28:08.186 --> 00:28:09.688
We found that these carpets

00:28:09.688 --> 00:28:11.523
were completely disintegrated,

00:28:11.523 --> 00:28:13.458
and they're definitely going
to have to replace them.

00:28:13.458 --> 00:28:15.093
And we actually haven't really seen

00:28:15.093 --> 00:28:17.429
carpets this bad before.

00:28:17.429 --> 00:28:19.564
So if I had to make an assumption

00:28:19.564 --> 00:28:21.399
on why this happened,

00:28:21.399 --> 00:28:24.903
it's a combination of
them sitting over time

00:28:24.903 --> 00:28:28.206
and the mice getting into
them and eating them up.

00:28:28.206 --> 00:28:30.742
And under the carpet, as
soon as we pulled it out,

00:28:30.742 --> 00:28:33.812
I looked down and found a nice surprise

00:28:33.812 --> 00:28:35.580
of a dead mouse.

00:28:35.580 --> 00:28:37.682
We disposed of it off camera,

00:28:37.682 --> 00:28:40.251
but we just had to pick
it up with our gloves

00:28:40.251 --> 00:28:43.021
and toss it in the garbage can.

00:28:43.021 --> 00:28:46.057
There's no nice way to
throw away a dead mouse.

00:28:47.158 --> 00:28:48.993
We started vacuuming the floor pans,

00:28:48.993 --> 00:28:52.764
and then we sanitized them
again with our TriClean.

00:29:07.545 --> 00:29:09.414
And then we used our steamer behind it

00:29:09.414 --> 00:29:11.182
to kill anything else.

00:29:13.518 --> 00:29:16.354
And then we wiped it all
away with microfiber towels.

00:29:18.957 --> 00:29:21.993
So, this dashboard was
covered in dust completely.

00:29:21.993 --> 00:29:23.828
And at this point we're
still using TriClean

00:29:23.828 --> 00:29:24.963
to clean the interior,

00:29:24.963 --> 00:29:26.931
because we want to disinfect everything.

00:29:26.931 --> 00:29:29.134
We knew that this car
isn't going to be started

00:29:29.134 --> 00:29:31.102
for quite a while, because it doesn't run

00:29:31.102 --> 00:29:32.871
and it needs a lot of repair work,

00:29:32.871 --> 00:29:35.206
so if cleaner gets inside the buttons

00:29:35.206 --> 00:29:37.108
or inside of the switches,

00:29:37.108 --> 00:29:38.843
it's not going to be the end of the world,

00:29:38.843 --> 00:29:40.712
because it'll definitely dry up before

00:29:40.712 --> 00:29:43.515
there's any kind of electricity
running through this car.

00:29:55.026 --> 00:29:57.862
So, if you want to get a barn find,

00:29:57.862 --> 00:30:01.132
a good way is searching
on Facebook Marketplace.

00:30:01.132 --> 00:30:02.333
The people that we work with

00:30:02.333 --> 00:30:04.269
specifically search for these cars,

00:30:04.269 --> 00:30:05.470
and then they bring them to us

00:30:05.470 --> 00:30:07.605
because they sat for so long.

00:30:07.605 --> 00:30:09.340
A lot of it's word of mouth.

00:30:18.850 --> 00:30:20.785
So, for these seats, they were covered in

00:30:20.785 --> 00:30:23.288
anything that the mice could put on them.

00:30:23.288 --> 00:30:26.090
So the first thing that
we do is vacuum them,

00:30:26.090 --> 00:30:27.792
and then we'll go ahead and extract them.

00:30:27.792 --> 00:30:29.861
So we use our extractor soap,

00:30:29.861 --> 00:30:32.697
and then we'll use our drill
brush to agitate the soap

00:30:32.697 --> 00:30:34.999
and really work it into those fibers.

00:30:34.999 --> 00:30:36.434
And then we'll use our extractor,

00:30:36.434 --> 00:30:38.336
which shoots hot water into the fibers

00:30:38.336 --> 00:30:40.138
and also sucks it away at the same time.

00:30:40.138 --> 00:30:43.274
So it's a vacuum, but
it's a vacuum with water.

00:30:49.581 --> 00:30:52.050
The exterior of this
car was covered in dust

00:30:52.050 --> 00:30:53.618
from front to back,

00:30:53.618 --> 00:30:55.720
and then there was some wood chips

00:30:55.720 --> 00:30:58.790
and other stuff in the headlight crevice,

00:30:58.790 --> 00:31:01.292
which, I'm pretty sure on this kit,

00:31:01.292 --> 00:31:03.161
there's supposed to be a headlight cover,

00:31:03.161 --> 00:31:04.896
but for some reason they were missing

00:31:04.896 --> 00:31:06.264
on this specific car.

00:31:12.003 --> 00:31:14.739
And here we're vacuuming
out some of the jambs,

00:31:14.739 --> 00:31:17.408
which we like to do before
we pressure wash off the car,

00:31:17.408 --> 00:31:19.410
because if there's any
wood chips or anything,

00:31:19.410 --> 00:31:21.246
it might scratch the paint.

00:31:23.314 --> 00:31:24.315
So, on the wheels,

00:31:24.315 --> 00:31:27.418
they were covered with some
sort of green substance,

00:31:27.418 --> 00:31:29.787
which, I don't know if it was mold or not.

00:31:29.787 --> 00:31:31.589
And to clean this tire, we'll go ahead

00:31:31.589 --> 00:31:34.292
and pressure wash
everything off of it first.

00:31:36.361 --> 00:31:38.930
And then we'll use our P&amp;S Brake Buster

00:31:38.930 --> 00:31:41.165
on the tire and the rim,

00:31:41.165 --> 00:31:43.001
and then we'll use an
assortment of brushes,

00:31:43.001 --> 00:31:45.803
usually soft-bristle
brushes and a tire brush.

00:31:45.803 --> 00:31:47.872
And then we'll pressure wash it all away.

00:31:59.017 --> 00:32:01.719
The paint on this car was
in pretty good condition.

00:32:01.719 --> 00:32:04.455
The only thing that we
noticed was deteriorating

00:32:04.455 --> 00:32:06.724
was the black paint.

00:32:06.724 --> 00:32:07.959
So, what we're spraying on this car

00:32:07.959 --> 00:32:09.894
is a paint-safe degreaser

00:32:09.894 --> 00:32:11.629
so we can loosen up all that dirt

00:32:11.629 --> 00:32:13.631
before we pressure wash it away.

00:32:13.631 --> 00:32:15.033
And what that's going to do

00:32:15.033 --> 00:32:18.536
is minimize any kind of
swirl marks or scratches.

00:32:21.005 --> 00:32:23.341
Paint chips can be damaging to a car,

00:32:23.341 --> 00:32:26.711
only because it can cause rust to form

00:32:26.711 --> 00:32:29.580
if there's metal underneath
where the paint was.

00:32:29.580 --> 00:32:31.849
But there was no rust on this body at all

00:32:31.849 --> 00:32:33.818
because it's made out of fiberglass.

00:32:39.791 --> 00:32:41.092
So, we're starting to foam the car here

00:32:41.092 --> 00:32:43.261
using our pressure washer and foam cannon.

00:32:43.261 --> 00:32:45.263
And this is the part that a lot of people

00:32:45.263 --> 00:32:48.032
find the most satisfying
in the whole process.

00:32:48.032 --> 00:32:50.435
And there's two reasons why
people use foam cannons.

00:32:50.435 --> 00:32:53.237
The first one is as a presoak,

00:32:53.237 --> 00:32:55.239
so they can loosen up all the dirt

00:32:55.239 --> 00:32:56.541
that's on the surface of the car.

00:32:56.541 --> 00:32:59.811
But also, instead of just
putting soap in a bucket,

00:32:59.811 --> 00:33:02.246
it helps you put soap over the entire car,

00:33:02.246 --> 00:33:06.084
which makes the process safer
for the paint and easier.

00:33:12.757 --> 00:33:13.958
And after foaming the car,

00:33:13.958 --> 00:33:16.694
we'll use microfiber
mitts on the entire car

00:33:16.694 --> 00:33:18.062
to wash it down.

00:33:33.344 --> 00:33:35.813
And now we're using our Rupes buffer

00:33:35.813 --> 00:33:37.548
to take any of the oxidation off

00:33:37.548 --> 00:33:39.384
the top layer of the paint,

00:33:39.384 --> 00:33:42.920
which will make it a
lot shinier, less dull,

00:33:42.920 --> 00:33:45.723
and give it that overall
wow factor at the end.

00:33:45.723 --> 00:33:47.558
So this is just, like, a paint correction.

00:33:47.558 --> 00:33:48.760
And when it came to the black paint,

00:33:48.760 --> 00:33:50.228
we didn't even polish it at all,

00:33:50.228 --> 00:33:52.964
because it was starting
to come off of the car

00:33:52.964 --> 00:33:55.833
and we didn't want to ruin or take away

00:33:55.833 --> 00:33:58.436
any of that paint that was already there.

00:33:58.436 --> 00:34:01.039
There wasn't too much that
we could personally do.

00:34:01.039 --> 00:34:03.374
It's something that would
have to either be redone

00:34:03.374 --> 00:34:05.076
or just left as is.

00:34:06.210 --> 00:34:07.712
And after the whole car is cleaned

00:34:07.712 --> 00:34:09.514
and we're ready to do
the finishing touches,

00:34:09.514 --> 00:34:11.349
we'll do a tire shine.

00:34:11.349 --> 00:34:13.618
And as that dries, it leaves the tires

00:34:13.618 --> 00:34:15.520
looking nice and matte black,

00:34:15.520 --> 00:34:18.222
and it just gives it
that overall wow factor

00:34:18.222 --> 00:34:19.791
for the customer.

00:34:19.791 --> 00:34:21.559
And the absolute last thing we'll do

00:34:21.559 --> 00:34:24.495
is spray a ceramic sealant
over the entire car.

00:34:24.495 --> 00:34:27.031
It'll protect the paint just a little bit,

00:34:27.031 --> 00:34:29.267
but it'll also give it a nice shine

00:34:29.267 --> 00:34:32.270
and just complete the detail.

00:34:32.270 --> 00:34:33.304
And that's it.

00:34:35.840 --> 00:34:38.476
I was super happy with the
way that this car turned out.

00:34:38.476 --> 00:34:41.112
I actually personally
want to buy it myself.

00:34:41.112 --> 00:34:43.448
We'll see how that goes
in the near future,

00:34:43.448 --> 00:34:45.883
when it's done being fixed up.

00:34:46.851 --> 00:34:48.453
When it comes to cleaning barn finds,

00:34:48.453 --> 00:34:50.188
if I can give any advice,

00:34:50.188 --> 00:34:51.856
it's just to take your time

00:34:51.856 --> 00:34:53.758
and really do your research.

00:34:53.758 --> 00:34:55.660
That way, you don't make any

00:34:55.660 --> 00:34:57.562
super-detrimental mistakes.

00:34:57.562 --> 00:34:59.330
Because the last thing you want to do

00:34:59.330 --> 00:35:01.532
is find something that has original paint

00:35:01.532 --> 00:35:05.636
or an original interior and damage it.

00:35:05.636 --> 00:35:08.172
But also, have fun with the process.

00:35:15.513 --> 00:35:18.416
Narrator: Next up, this
car has seen better days

00:35:18.416 --> 00:35:21.719
as 22 years of mold is deep cleaned.

00:35:22.487 --> 00:35:23.855
Hi, my name is Brent Dicesare,

00:35:23.855 --> 00:35:25.656
I'm one half of WD Detailing,

00:35:25.656 --> 00:35:27.291
and today we're going to be cleaning

00:35:27.291 --> 00:35:29.193
a really moldy Buick Reatta

00:35:29.193 --> 00:35:32.396
that sat outside for over 22 years.

00:35:32.396 --> 00:35:34.799
The owner took me into his backyard,

00:35:34.799 --> 00:35:37.235
and as soon as I saw
this one specifically,

00:35:37.235 --> 00:35:38.803
I knew we had to do it.

00:35:38.803 --> 00:35:40.571
When we opened the doors, the first thing

00:35:40.571 --> 00:35:43.107
that we found was a ton of mold

00:35:43.107 --> 00:35:45.476
and what looks like a mouse nest

00:35:45.476 --> 00:35:47.445
beginning to form at some point.

00:35:48.412 --> 00:35:51.916
All in all, this car smelled
absolutely horrendous,

00:35:51.916 --> 00:35:54.619
and it was just disgusting.

00:35:58.256 --> 00:35:59.590
So, the first thing that we're doing

00:35:59.590 --> 00:36:01.526
is working on the engine bay,

00:36:01.526 --> 00:36:03.261
and that means getting rid of

00:36:03.261 --> 00:36:06.097
all of the leaves and
anything that piled up

00:36:06.097 --> 00:36:08.566
over the years in the jamb of the bay.

00:36:08.566 --> 00:36:10.568
After that, we went ahead and we used

00:36:10.568 --> 00:36:13.504
a pressure washer and
some degreaser on it.

00:36:13.504 --> 00:36:15.106
And after we finish the engine bay,

00:36:15.106 --> 00:36:16.474
we moved on to the wheels.

00:36:16.474 --> 00:36:17.341
So, when cleaning the wheels,

00:36:17.341 --> 00:36:19.577
the first thing we'll do
is pressure wash them off,

00:36:19.577 --> 00:36:21.412
just to get a cleaner surface.

00:36:23.214 --> 00:36:26.484
And then we'll spray our
wheel and tire cleaner on them

00:36:26.484 --> 00:36:28.152
and use a couple different brushes

00:36:28.152 --> 00:36:30.755
to get into the wheel crevices

00:36:30.755 --> 00:36:32.390
and on the face of the rim

00:36:32.390 --> 00:36:35.326
and then behind the rim and in the barrel.

00:36:35.326 --> 00:36:37.261
Then, after that, we'll
spray everything away,

00:36:37.261 --> 00:36:39.263
leaving a really clean wheel.

00:36:40.932 --> 00:36:42.533
Now we're getting ready to pressure wash

00:36:42.533 --> 00:36:44.702
and clean the outside of the car.

00:36:44.702 --> 00:36:47.405
We wanted to use our
paint-safe degreaser first

00:36:47.405 --> 00:36:50.374
to loosen up all that mold and grime

00:36:50.374 --> 00:36:53.544
before we actually took a
pressure washer to the surface.

00:36:53.544 --> 00:36:55.046
And then we started pressure washing,

00:36:55.046 --> 00:36:57.415
which is by far the most satisfying part

00:36:57.415 --> 00:36:59.817
of any detail that we do.

00:36:59.817 --> 00:37:01.519
So, our process with pressure washing,

00:37:01.519 --> 00:37:04.222
we always like to try to
work from top to bottom.

00:37:04.222 --> 00:37:06.591
So the first thing we did was the roof.

00:37:06.591 --> 00:37:08.926
So we went to the hood after that,

00:37:08.926 --> 00:37:10.461
and then the sides,

00:37:11.362 --> 00:37:12.997
and then the rear.

00:37:14.065 --> 00:37:16.867
Just watching the pressure
washer go in straight lines,

00:37:16.867 --> 00:37:19.770
taking away those years of grime,

00:37:19.770 --> 00:37:22.340
just feels so good inside.

00:37:22.340 --> 00:37:24.208
I can't even explain it.

00:37:26.477 --> 00:37:29.213
And then, after having all
that pressure washed off,

00:37:29.213 --> 00:37:32.250
we'll go ahead and spray
the entire car down

00:37:32.250 --> 00:37:34.218
with our foam cannon.

00:37:34.218 --> 00:37:35.987
So, here we're using the foam cannon,

00:37:35.987 --> 00:37:37.488
and the reason for that is because

00:37:37.488 --> 00:37:38.990
after pressure washing the car,

00:37:38.990 --> 00:37:41.225
there is still grime and dirt left

00:37:41.225 --> 00:37:42.927
on the surface of the paint,

00:37:42.927 --> 00:37:46.631
and the foam cannon really
just helps add more soap

00:37:46.631 --> 00:37:49.934
to the paint than putting
soap in a bucket would.

00:37:49.934 --> 00:37:51.869
And when you're washing the car off

00:37:51.869 --> 00:37:53.371
with microfiber mitts,

00:37:53.371 --> 00:37:56.107
it makes it easier to do, and quicker.

00:37:58.175 --> 00:38:01.012
After we wash it off, we
spray the ceramic spray,

00:38:01.012 --> 00:38:02.446
just to give it some protection

00:38:02.446 --> 00:38:04.248
before moving on to the interior.

00:38:09.854 --> 00:38:12.156
So, in our first round of vacuuming,

00:38:12.156 --> 00:38:14.859
we knew that this car was really bad,

00:38:14.859 --> 00:38:16.827
so we weren't going to get everything.

00:38:16.827 --> 00:38:18.729
So we definitely were going to need to use

00:38:18.729 --> 00:38:22.566
the drill brush and extractor later on.

00:38:23.868 --> 00:38:27.505
This area looks like mice made a nest,

00:38:27.505 --> 00:38:31.442
and they were both leaving
their droppings behind

00:38:31.442 --> 00:38:35.413
and also bringing some
food or different materials

00:38:35.413 --> 00:38:37.682
from the inside of the
car that they could find.

00:38:37.682 --> 00:38:39.016
We were able to vacuum that up

00:38:39.016 --> 00:38:41.585
and actually get it pretty clean.

00:38:41.585 --> 00:38:44.455
So, when we work on
doors that have leather

00:38:44.455 --> 00:38:46.924
or vinyl or anything like that,

00:38:46.924 --> 00:38:50.027
we're pretty much using the same process,

00:38:50.027 --> 00:38:51.929
especially when they're covered in mold.

00:38:51.929 --> 00:38:54.098
The first thing we'll do is use Tri-Clean,

00:38:54.098 --> 00:38:58.336
which is a cleaner that
kills 99.9% of bacteria,

00:38:58.336 --> 00:39:00.838
and then we'll use soft-bristle brushes

00:39:00.838 --> 00:39:03.107
and sometimes a steamer from a distance,

00:39:03.107 --> 00:39:05.810
just to kill the bacteria
and kill the mold,

00:39:05.810 --> 00:39:07.478
and then we'll wipe it away.

00:39:07.478 --> 00:39:09.480
And we'll spend a decent amount of time

00:39:09.480 --> 00:39:10.781
on each section of the car

00:39:10.781 --> 00:39:12.616
to make sure that we really get it clean

00:39:12.616 --> 00:39:14.685
before moving on to the next one.

00:39:14.685 --> 00:39:16.887
We repeat the process with the steamer

00:39:16.887 --> 00:39:19.890
and the Tri-Clean throughout
the entire interior,

00:39:19.890 --> 00:39:21.258
including the steering wheel,

00:39:21.258 --> 00:39:24.395
which was covered in a
very thick layer of mold,

00:39:24.395 --> 00:39:27.765
worse than any steering
wheel we've ever seen.

00:39:27.765 --> 00:39:30.000
So, what we're using is the Tornador,

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:31.936
and that's pressurized air,

00:39:31.936 --> 00:39:34.271
and it's using it to dry it off

00:39:34.271 --> 00:39:37.942
and also blow out any
product that could be

00:39:37.942 --> 00:39:39.777
in the cracks or anything like that.

00:39:39.777 --> 00:39:42.346
Older leather can definitely
be tougher to work with,

00:39:42.346 --> 00:39:45.116
because if mold sits
on it for a long time,

00:39:45.116 --> 00:39:48.285
it can damage it, it can remove the dye,

00:39:48.285 --> 00:39:50.688
and you also have to be really
careful with the steamer,

00:39:50.688 --> 00:39:53.491
because if you spend too
much time in one area

00:39:53.491 --> 00:39:56.660
or you get too close, it
can definitely damage it.

00:39:57.928 --> 00:39:59.630
And after we clean the
interior components,

00:39:59.630 --> 00:40:01.632
we'll go ahead and focus on the carpets,

00:40:01.632 --> 00:40:02.633
and the first thing we'll do

00:40:02.633 --> 00:40:05.603
is spray our extractor
soap into the carpet

00:40:05.603 --> 00:40:07.171
and we'll get it wet,

00:40:07.171 --> 00:40:08.339
and then we'll use the drill brush

00:40:08.339 --> 00:40:10.674
to agitate it into the
fibers of the carpet

00:40:10.674 --> 00:40:14.845
to help release any stains
or any dirt that's in there.

00:40:14.845 --> 00:40:18.149
And then we'll use the
extractor to suck that all away.

00:40:26.457 --> 00:40:28.359
And after the interior's completely clean

00:40:28.359 --> 00:40:30.828
with the leather and the
vinyl, anything like that,

00:40:30.828 --> 00:40:32.930
we'll go ahead and use
a leather conditioner.

00:40:32.930 --> 00:40:35.166
That way, it can bring back the softness

00:40:35.166 --> 00:40:36.467
and the suppleness,

00:40:36.467 --> 00:40:38.869
but it also leaves it with a nice shine.

00:40:41.572 --> 00:40:43.374
And then some of the
final things that we do

00:40:43.374 --> 00:40:45.910
to the car is cleaning the windows,

00:40:48.979 --> 00:40:50.481
and we add a tire shine

00:40:50.481 --> 00:40:52.583
to give the car those finishing touches,

00:40:52.583 --> 00:40:54.585
so when the owner actually sees it,

00:40:54.585 --> 00:40:56.086
it gives that wow factor.

00:40:56.086 --> 00:40:58.422
All in all, this detail probably took us

00:40:58.422 --> 00:41:01.625
around 12 hours over
the course of one day.

00:41:01.625 --> 00:41:03.894
And just looking over the
final result of the car,

00:41:03.894 --> 00:41:05.129
we couldn't have been happier

00:41:05.129 --> 00:41:06.096
with the way it turned out.

00:41:06.096 --> 00:41:07.431
Even though there was some damage

00:41:07.431 --> 00:41:09.433
to the exterior with the paint,

00:41:09.433 --> 00:41:12.670
everything else absolutely was fantastic.

00:41:25.716 --> 00:41:28.118
Narrator: Andreas Yurich
from WorkShop Rebuild

00:41:28.118 --> 00:41:30.187
shares how he uses a laser

00:41:30.187 --> 00:41:32.690
to remove years of rust from this old car.

00:41:34.425 --> 00:41:36.126
Hi. My name's Andreas Yurich.

00:41:36.126 --> 00:41:38.162
On social media, I go by Workshop Rebuild,

00:41:38.162 --> 00:41:40.097
and today I'll be sharing with you guys

00:41:40.097 --> 00:41:41.599
the laser-cleaning process.

00:41:41.599 --> 00:41:43.534
And I will be cleaning 40 years of rust

00:41:43.534 --> 00:41:46.504
on a classic car from 1979.

00:41:46.504 --> 00:41:48.706
Laser cleaning is a
process where a laser beam

00:41:48.706 --> 00:41:51.542
gets emitted from a
laser-cleaning machine.

00:41:51.542 --> 00:41:54.378
And this handheld device
will always be pointed

00:41:54.378 --> 00:41:58.449
towards a metal surface with
any surface contaminants.

00:41:58.449 --> 00:42:00.251
If you have parts that come in

00:42:00.251 --> 00:42:02.486
that are just full of grease, full of oil,

00:42:02.486 --> 00:42:05.689
just full of any surface contamination,

00:42:05.689 --> 00:42:07.858
you can actually use this
laser-cleaning process

00:42:07.858 --> 00:42:09.693
to remove all of that.

00:42:10.995 --> 00:42:13.664
Looking at the Renault R5, Le Car,

00:42:13.664 --> 00:42:15.499
the bodywork is still very straight,

00:42:15.499 --> 00:42:17.568
but around the edges,
it does have some rust.

00:42:17.568 --> 00:42:18.936
And especially on the underside

00:42:18.936 --> 00:42:20.237
or around the wheel wells,

00:42:20.237 --> 00:42:21.805
you will see quite a bit of rust.

00:42:21.805 --> 00:42:23.340
And I would like to remove that,

00:42:23.340 --> 00:42:26.143
so I can bring this car
back to running conditions

00:42:26.143 --> 00:42:29.346
and also make it somehow
suitable for the road.

00:42:29.346 --> 00:42:30.948
The first step is just really

00:42:30.948 --> 00:42:32.883
visually looking at everything.

00:42:32.883 --> 00:42:34.018
It's really important to know

00:42:34.018 --> 00:42:35.352
where the rust is building up

00:42:35.352 --> 00:42:37.087
and which direction it's going in

00:42:37.087 --> 00:42:39.723
to actually address it
with the laser cleaner.

00:42:39.723 --> 00:42:41.058
The next step is actually just

00:42:41.058 --> 00:42:43.794
to prepare the laser-cleaning machine.

00:42:43.794 --> 00:42:45.396
Once I have all the settings set up,

00:42:45.396 --> 00:42:46.897
I will put on my protective gear.

00:42:46.897 --> 00:42:48.265
So I will be wearing a mask,

00:42:48.265 --> 00:42:51.368
and I will be wearing
laser-protective glasses,

00:42:51.368 --> 00:42:52.436
just to be safe.

00:42:52.436 --> 00:42:54.238
After that, I grab the handheld gun,

00:42:54.238 --> 00:42:55.973
and I get to work.

00:42:55.973 --> 00:42:57.575
And I started off with the wheels.

00:42:57.575 --> 00:42:59.476
I'm taking away quite a bit of rust,

00:42:59.476 --> 00:43:00.978
and that's been built up

00:43:00.978 --> 00:43:02.947
for over 40 years, right?

00:43:02.947 --> 00:43:04.882
So, the most outer parts of the car

00:43:04.882 --> 00:43:06.150
I wanted to clean first,

00:43:06.150 --> 00:43:07.818
and I was going to work my way in.

00:43:07.818 --> 00:43:09.219
That was just my thought process.

00:43:09.219 --> 00:43:11.822
Can be in any other order,

00:43:11.822 --> 00:43:13.624
but that's just the way I do things.

00:43:13.624 --> 00:43:14.792
After the wheels were clean,

00:43:14.792 --> 00:43:16.827
I did the fenders themselves.

00:43:16.827 --> 00:43:18.228
Right around the fenders it rusts,

00:43:18.228 --> 00:43:19.630
because you have, all the time,

00:43:19.630 --> 00:43:21.732
water splashing up on the fender.

00:43:21.732 --> 00:43:22.833
And that's where it will rust,

00:43:22.833 --> 00:43:25.102
because the water
actually doesn't evaporate

00:43:25.102 --> 00:43:27.638
or go away, it just
hides behind the fender.

00:43:30.341 --> 00:43:32.009
Once I was done with the fenders,

00:43:32.009 --> 00:43:34.845
I moved in to the wheel hubs.

00:43:34.845 --> 00:43:36.313
It cleaned up the rust very well.

00:43:36.313 --> 00:43:39.316
I was really surprised that
it cleaned up everything.

00:43:40.784 --> 00:43:44.622
Cleaned up the threads
on the hubs themselves.

00:43:47.191 --> 00:43:50.127
So, how does laser cleaning actually work?

00:43:50.127 --> 00:43:52.830
So the laser-cleaning machine
has a certain frequency.

00:43:52.830 --> 00:43:54.765
Once its frequency is established

00:43:54.765 --> 00:43:56.967
within the laser source,

00:43:56.967 --> 00:43:59.403
that will be emitted
through the handheld gun.

00:43:59.403 --> 00:44:01.772
Once it's pointed at your work piece,

00:44:01.772 --> 00:44:04.441
it will resonate with the contamination

00:44:04.441 --> 00:44:05.876
on top of the metal surface.

00:44:05.876 --> 00:44:08.012
The metal surface is the last resort.

00:44:08.012 --> 00:44:09.913
That will not absorb any light.

00:44:09.913 --> 00:44:12.216
So everything above the metal surface

00:44:12.216 --> 00:44:13.917
will actually absorb the light

00:44:13.917 --> 00:44:15.919
of the laser-cleaning machine.

00:44:15.919 --> 00:44:19.156
Once it touches anything above the metal,

00:44:19.156 --> 00:44:22.292
the heat will actually
remove the contamination

00:44:22.292 --> 00:44:23.293
on top of the metal.

00:44:23.293 --> 00:44:25.929
Either that, if it's not
the pressure or the heat,

00:44:25.929 --> 00:44:27.264
the laser beam itself will

00:44:27.264 --> 00:44:30.067
vaporize the substance on top, right?

00:44:30.067 --> 00:44:31.568
So it's either or, right?

00:44:31.568 --> 00:44:33.237
And that happens within

00:44:33.237 --> 00:44:35.305
milliseconds. Nanoseconds. So.

00:44:35.305 --> 00:44:37.474
With any laser-cleaning machine,

00:44:37.474 --> 00:44:38.676
this is a beam of light,

00:44:38.676 --> 00:44:40.377
and it also creates a lot of heat.

00:44:40.377 --> 00:44:42.613
You can damage the surface substrate,

00:44:42.613 --> 00:44:43.580
which is the metal.

00:44:43.580 --> 00:44:45.983
So you always want to keep your tool

00:44:45.983 --> 00:44:47.985
or your handheld gun in motion.

00:44:47.985 --> 00:44:49.920
You do not want to keep it too long

00:44:49.920 --> 00:44:52.022
at one certain area or on one spot

00:44:52.022 --> 00:44:54.358
because you can damage the metal

00:44:54.358 --> 00:44:57.094
if you keep it on one spot for too long.

00:44:57.094 --> 00:44:59.430
Once I finished up with the wheel hubs,

00:44:59.430 --> 00:45:00.931
I actually moved on to the muffler.

00:45:00.931 --> 00:45:02.833
That was on the other side.

00:45:02.833 --> 00:45:05.302
There are exactly four
settings on this machine

00:45:05.302 --> 00:45:06.904
that I can manipulate.

00:45:06.904 --> 00:45:08.739
So, the first setting is power.

00:45:08.739 --> 00:45:10.974
The second setting is frequency.

00:45:10.974 --> 00:45:14.044
The third setting is the speed
of the laser beam itself,

00:45:14.044 --> 00:45:17.347
and the fourth setting is
the width of the laser beam.

00:45:17.347 --> 00:45:18.515
So with all these settings,

00:45:18.515 --> 00:45:21.952
I can vary or adjust the laser beam.

00:45:21.952 --> 00:45:23.654
So if I'm cleaning aluminum,

00:45:23.654 --> 00:45:25.723
I will bring the settings
a little bit down,

00:45:25.723 --> 00:45:27.491
because aluminum is softer

00:45:27.491 --> 00:45:30.627
and it also has a lower
melting temperature.

00:45:32.196 --> 00:45:34.531
For steel, I go up in the settings,

00:45:34.531 --> 00:45:37.901
and usually I run them at around 80%,

00:45:37.901 --> 00:45:41.138
and aluminum I run at 60%.

00:45:41.138 --> 00:45:43.307
I really noticed along the body panels,

00:45:43.307 --> 00:45:45.242
I have to be very delicate.

00:45:45.242 --> 00:45:47.344
I had to bring down the
values a little bit.

00:45:47.344 --> 00:45:49.146
Because that metal is so thin,

00:45:49.146 --> 00:45:52.216
I just had to keep it in
movement all the time.

00:45:53.217 --> 00:45:55.953
And lastly, I ended up around the engine,

00:45:55.953 --> 00:45:59.089
where some components
were already missing,

00:45:59.089 --> 00:46:01.959
but I obviously cleaned up
the inside of the engine.

00:46:01.959 --> 00:46:03.460
So, you can see the before and after

00:46:03.460 --> 00:46:05.829
on all four cylinders.

00:46:05.829 --> 00:46:07.865
The cylinders cleaned up very well.

00:46:07.865 --> 00:46:09.933
The cylinder head, the intake manifold,

00:46:09.933 --> 00:46:11.535
and the exhaust manifold were removed

00:46:11.535 --> 00:46:13.003
from the car previously,

00:46:13.003 --> 00:46:15.172
so I could clean them up on the bench.

00:46:15.172 --> 00:46:16.840
And cleaning them up on the bench

00:46:16.840 --> 00:46:18.509
actually gives me the opportunity

00:46:18.509 --> 00:46:19.977
to clean them all the way around,

00:46:19.977 --> 00:46:21.745
which makes it super easy.

00:46:21.745 --> 00:46:24.047
And the final finish also shows

00:46:24.047 --> 00:46:26.917
that it's a much better way
to clean them on the bench.

00:46:28.152 --> 00:46:29.253
What's really nice about it

00:46:29.253 --> 00:46:31.054
is that you do not damage the substrate,

00:46:31.054 --> 00:46:32.589
which is the metal surface.

00:46:32.589 --> 00:46:34.792
So if you're working on machined areas,

00:46:34.792 --> 00:46:37.394
like internal components for an engine,

00:46:37.394 --> 00:46:39.029
anything around any bodywork

00:46:39.029 --> 00:46:42.099
for a very, very delicate
restoration project,

00:46:42.099 --> 00:46:43.967
or even something historical,

00:46:43.967 --> 00:46:45.669
you do not want to damage that substrate.

00:46:45.669 --> 00:46:48.505
So that's where laser cleaning comes in.

00:46:48.505 --> 00:46:50.307
So, the technology behind laser cleaning

00:46:50.307 --> 00:46:52.109
is evolving very fast.

00:46:52.109 --> 00:46:53.944
A lot of companies or manufacturers

00:46:53.944 --> 00:46:56.914
are starting to hook these up to robots

00:46:56.914 --> 00:46:59.283
and to their production lines.

00:46:59.283 --> 00:47:01.518
Even after something is manufactured,

00:47:01.518 --> 00:47:04.588
in any industry, there
is still some residue,

00:47:04.588 --> 00:47:07.124
debris, or something
that has to be removed

00:47:07.124 --> 00:47:08.759
for a further process.

00:47:08.759 --> 00:47:11.395
So when I look at the
final result of this car,

00:47:11.395 --> 00:47:13.931
from front to back or outside to in,

00:47:13.931 --> 00:47:16.834
I'm really happy and
satisfied with the finish.

00:47:16.834 --> 00:47:18.969
The body panels cleaned up very well.

00:47:18.969 --> 00:47:21.638
It removed all the paint.
It removed all the primer.

00:47:21.638 --> 00:47:23.440
It even removed some of the rust

00:47:23.440 --> 00:47:25.342
that was actually even chipping away.

00:47:25.342 --> 00:47:26.643
And towards the front of the engine,

00:47:26.643 --> 00:47:28.178
where I have aluminum parts,

00:47:28.178 --> 00:47:29.546
it just cleaned that perfect,

00:47:29.546 --> 00:47:33.150
without any issues and
without any thermal damage.

00:47:33.150 --> 00:47:35.118
I was really happy with everything,

00:47:35.118 --> 00:47:38.589
and now I can continue on
with my rebuild process.

00:47:38.589 --> 00:47:41.124
Since this technology is really new,

00:47:41.124 --> 00:47:42.559
I'm still learning as I'm going,

00:47:42.559 --> 00:47:46.263
so I'm not a true professional just yet,

00:47:46.263 --> 00:47:47.764
but I'm learning every day.

00:47:47.764 --> 00:47:50.334
I'm really into this new technology,

00:47:50.334 --> 00:47:52.669
and I can't wait for this technology

00:47:52.669 --> 00:47:54.438
to get better in the near future.

00:47:57.474 --> 00:47:59.977
Narrator: Dryce Nation
takes it one step further

00:47:59.977 --> 00:48:04.081
with the latest detailing
technology, dry ice.

00:48:07.718 --> 00:48:09.386
It's Scott Ales with Dryce Nation.

00:48:09.386 --> 00:48:10.554
So, today I'd like to show you

00:48:10.554 --> 00:48:11.955
the step-by-step process

00:48:11.955 --> 00:48:14.558
that we go through in dry-ice cleaning.

00:48:15.726 --> 00:48:16.793
So, there's a number of things

00:48:16.793 --> 00:48:18.462
that are unique about dry-ice cleaning.

00:48:18.462 --> 00:48:20.697
No. 1, we're not using water,

00:48:20.697 --> 00:48:23.634
so when we're done, we're
not cleaning up water spots,

00:48:23.634 --> 00:48:25.569
we're not trying to dry things.

00:48:25.569 --> 00:48:27.571
If you can see the dirt, we can clean it.

00:48:27.571 --> 00:48:28.705
So there's a lot of places

00:48:28.705 --> 00:48:30.807
where you might be able to see that dirt

00:48:30.807 --> 00:48:33.043
but you can't physically get to it

00:48:33.043 --> 00:48:36.413
with any tools or brushes or devices,

00:48:36.413 --> 00:48:37.781
and we can.

00:48:37.781 --> 00:48:39.950
We can clean that in the
deep recesses of your car

00:48:39.950 --> 00:48:42.152
that you might not have
been able to before.

00:48:42.152 --> 00:48:44.888
It's like when the car
rolled off the factory line.

00:48:44.888 --> 00:48:47.758
You've cleaned it back to that condition.

00:48:47.758 --> 00:48:49.660
So, when we're just getting started,

00:48:49.660 --> 00:48:51.929
we're trying to identify the method

00:48:51.929 --> 00:48:53.630
of what we're going to attack first.

00:48:53.630 --> 00:48:55.065
When we put a car on the lift,

00:48:55.065 --> 00:48:55.999
we always want to make sure

00:48:55.999 --> 00:48:56.900
and pull the wheels off

00:48:56.900 --> 00:49:00.370
so we can inspect the
inner wheelhouse area.

00:49:00.370 --> 00:49:04.141
We use a torque wrench to
break the lug nuts loose.

00:49:04.141 --> 00:49:06.877
We also like to consider,
depending on the customer,

00:49:06.877 --> 00:49:10.414
if they want us to remove
the inner wheelhouse liners,

00:49:10.414 --> 00:49:12.149
which are typically plastic,

00:49:12.149 --> 00:49:14.785
to see if they want us
to clean behind there.

00:49:14.785 --> 00:49:17.054
So once we have the car on
the lift and we're prepped,

00:49:17.054 --> 00:49:19.189
we're then moving our attention

00:49:19.189 --> 00:49:21.391
toward the compressed-air system

00:49:21.391 --> 00:49:22.726
to make sure that we've got

00:49:22.726 --> 00:49:24.962
all of our drying and cleaning systems

00:49:24.962 --> 00:49:26.496
for the air system in order,

00:49:26.496 --> 00:49:28.165
and then we're going to load the machine

00:49:28.165 --> 00:49:29.132
with the dry ice

00:49:29.132 --> 00:49:31.268
so that we're prepared to blast.

00:49:34.771 --> 00:49:35.973
And so the first thing we try to do

00:49:35.973 --> 00:49:36.940
is try to get the wheels out of the way.

00:49:36.940 --> 00:49:37.975
We have four wheels, and we'll just

00:49:37.975 --> 00:49:40.444
take those one at a time.

00:49:40.444 --> 00:49:43.080
We'll look for tar,
we'll look for adhesion,

00:49:43.080 --> 00:49:44.881
remnants from old wheel weights,

00:49:44.881 --> 00:49:46.516
which are totally a pain to remove,

00:49:46.516 --> 00:49:48.652
and we make that pretty easy.

00:49:48.652 --> 00:49:51.388
And then we'll just clean
the wheels inside and out.

00:49:54.124 --> 00:49:56.193
The three settings are,
you've got pressure,

00:49:56.193 --> 00:49:58.462
which is psi that you're
all familiar with;

00:49:58.462 --> 00:50:00.497
there's pounds per minute;

00:50:00.497 --> 00:50:02.399
and then you've got size of particle.

00:50:02.399 --> 00:50:04.701
So those are the three
things that you can adjust.

00:50:04.701 --> 00:50:05.535
But for the first time

00:50:05.535 --> 00:50:07.337
we can actually choose
the size of the particle,

00:50:07.337 --> 00:50:10.040
so it enables us to be
able do delicate things

00:50:10.040 --> 00:50:14.478
in the same area of things
that might be aggressive.

00:50:14.478 --> 00:50:17.914
Previously you were putting
those delicate items at risk

00:50:17.914 --> 00:50:20.083
with the large particle size.

00:50:20.083 --> 00:50:21.651
When we're starting the project,

00:50:21.651 --> 00:50:24.955
we're always looking for
the dirtiest area first.

00:50:24.955 --> 00:50:26.790
We like to get the hard
stuff out of the way,

00:50:26.790 --> 00:50:30.360
so that when we get to those
surfaces or those coatings

00:50:30.360 --> 00:50:32.229
that we're trying to remove that are easy,

00:50:32.229 --> 00:50:34.131
then it makes everything go smoother

00:50:34.131 --> 00:50:35.599
because you're not double cleaning.

00:50:35.599 --> 00:50:37.234
So, you'll notice as I'm cleaning

00:50:37.234 --> 00:50:39.870
that the gun is never static.

00:50:39.870 --> 00:50:40.771
It's always moving.

00:50:40.771 --> 00:50:42.472
It's always constantly moving.

00:50:42.472 --> 00:50:44.374
And the reason for that is we have

00:50:44.374 --> 00:50:48.812
the risk of a concentrated -109 degrees

00:50:48.812 --> 00:50:51.915
on a particular substrate
or part of the car

00:50:51.915 --> 00:50:54.651
that you could damage in one spot.

00:50:54.651 --> 00:50:55.585
So, I'd like to take a minute

00:50:55.585 --> 00:50:57.821
and explain how this actually works.

00:50:57.821 --> 00:50:59.956
There's really three
things that are occurring.

00:50:59.956 --> 00:51:01.124
You've got kinetic energy,

00:51:01.124 --> 00:51:04.261
which is driven by the
psi of the air system.

00:51:04.261 --> 00:51:06.963
You've got cold temperature, -109 degree,

00:51:06.963 --> 00:51:09.633
that causes things to shrink
so they lose adhesion.

00:51:09.633 --> 00:51:12.702
And then you've got the
actual thermal expansion

00:51:12.702 --> 00:51:15.338
of a solid chemical going to a gas,

00:51:15.338 --> 00:51:18.341
so that's 800 times its
original solid size.

00:51:18.341 --> 00:51:21.311
Those three things work very well together

00:51:21.311 --> 00:51:22.546
to remove this dirt in a way

00:51:22.546 --> 00:51:25.015
that you've probably never seen before.

00:51:27.317 --> 00:51:29.586
There's a lot of technique
opportunity here, right?

00:51:29.586 --> 00:51:30.687
'Cause in a sense,

00:51:30.687 --> 00:51:34.524
this is a bit of an artist's effort

00:51:34.524 --> 00:51:36.560
at an industrial job, if you will.

00:51:36.560 --> 00:51:39.529
I've found it to be helpful for me

00:51:39.529 --> 00:51:42.799
to function in a circular pattern

00:51:42.799 --> 00:51:45.302
so that I never have any hard lines.

00:51:45.302 --> 00:51:48.805
The circular pattern gives me the control

00:51:48.805 --> 00:51:50.740
that I feel comfortable with.

00:51:51.775 --> 00:51:54.177
There's really nothing that's more fun

00:51:54.177 --> 00:51:56.980
in this process than doing bare aluminum.

00:51:58.148 --> 00:52:01.551
You really can't hurt bare aluminum,

00:52:01.551 --> 00:52:04.321
and so in that case, you can crank it up,

00:52:04.321 --> 00:52:06.756
it's more efficient, you
feel better about it,

00:52:06.756 --> 00:52:10.727
and it's very satisfying to the eye.

00:52:12.162 --> 00:52:13.230
So, once we've removed

00:52:13.230 --> 00:52:17.968
those really difficult grimy surfaces,

00:52:17.968 --> 00:52:22.405
we do move into a combination of handwork

00:52:22.405 --> 00:52:25.842
and fine-detail dry-ice cleaning.

00:52:25.842 --> 00:52:27.944
People presume that what we're doing

00:52:27.944 --> 00:52:28.979
is a magic wand

00:52:28.979 --> 00:52:31.214
and we just wave it once and we're done,

00:52:31.214 --> 00:52:33.517
and that's not true.

00:52:33.517 --> 00:52:35.519
When we have surface rust,

00:52:35.519 --> 00:52:38.755
we do integrate a fair amount of handwork.

00:52:38.755 --> 00:52:40.690
Whether it be all-purpose cleaners,

00:52:40.690 --> 00:52:44.494
white erasers, Ospho, 0000 steel wool,

00:52:44.494 --> 00:52:47.631
definitely we do use handwork.

00:52:47.631 --> 00:52:49.232
At the point in time that we believe

00:52:49.232 --> 00:52:50.767
we're finished with the underside,

00:52:50.767 --> 00:52:52.736
we'll bring the car
down three or four feet,

00:52:52.736 --> 00:52:54.571
and then we'll look at
the top of the suspension

00:52:54.571 --> 00:52:56.206
and the wheelhouse areas
and then we'll notice,

00:52:56.206 --> 00:52:58.308
"OK, we've got some more work to do here."

00:52:58.308 --> 00:53:00.043
So once we've cleaned
the underside of the car,

00:53:00.043 --> 00:53:01.344
we bring the car down on the lift,

00:53:01.344 --> 00:53:04.247
and we have an extra-wide lift

00:53:04.247 --> 00:53:05.615
so that we can open the doors,

00:53:05.615 --> 00:53:06.917
get the floor mats out,

00:53:06.917 --> 00:53:08.618
we can do the interior vents,

00:53:08.618 --> 00:53:11.121
the door jams, the engine bay, the trim.

00:53:11.121 --> 00:53:12.923
And we don't abrade the surface,

00:53:12.923 --> 00:53:16.526
and we'll do what takes somebody
two hours in 10 minutes.

00:53:23.533 --> 00:53:24.935
So, we were fortunate enough

00:53:24.935 --> 00:53:27.404
to receive this '55 Porsche Speedster,

00:53:27.404 --> 00:53:28.572
and it literally is a barn find.

00:53:28.572 --> 00:53:31.041
What you're seeing here
is me trying to see

00:53:31.041 --> 00:53:34.644
if I can remove this crazy-thick mold

00:53:34.644 --> 00:53:39.583
that's 30, 40 years old
off 60-year-old vinyl.

00:53:39.583 --> 00:53:43.720
That mold was so thick it
would stop your fingernail.

00:53:43.720 --> 00:53:45.088
This was a big moment for us,

00:53:45.088 --> 00:53:46.523
and the end result is

00:53:46.523 --> 00:53:49.926
we will apply some all-purpose
cleaner at the end.

00:53:49.926 --> 00:53:54.097
and we'll wipe off the
final film from this panel.

00:53:54.097 --> 00:53:56.433
But it just turns out spectacular.

00:53:59.202 --> 00:54:02.806
So, we don't add to or remove any CO2

00:54:02.806 --> 00:54:05.041
from the environment, so we're neutral.

00:54:05.041 --> 00:54:07.110
And we don't use water, which means

00:54:07.110 --> 00:54:10.146
that we don't rinse chemicals down a drain

00:54:10.146 --> 00:54:14.351
into any sewer systems
or into drain fields.

00:54:14.351 --> 00:54:16.586
So from an environmental standpoint,

00:54:16.586 --> 00:54:20.023
by far it is absolutely

00:54:20.023 --> 00:54:22.425
a better cleaning solution

00:54:22.425 --> 00:54:25.262
than using water and chemicals.

00:54:25.262 --> 00:54:27.030
The dry-ice-cleaning world

00:54:27.030 --> 00:54:31.134
could be considered the
highest-level OCD crowd.

00:54:31.134 --> 00:54:33.937
Think about the underside of a car

00:54:33.937 --> 00:54:37.807
and the intricacies of every component.

00:54:37.807 --> 00:54:39.342
There's a lot of surface area

00:54:39.342 --> 00:54:41.411
to all of these parts and pieces.

00:54:41.411 --> 00:54:44.247
Don't think that it's going to be easy,

00:54:44.247 --> 00:54:46.249
but the satisfaction factor

00:54:46.249 --> 00:54:48.485
for those that care about the way

00:54:48.485 --> 00:54:51.154
their assets or their artifacts look

00:54:51.154 --> 00:54:52.622
is at the highest level.

00:54:52.622 --> 00:54:54.190
So it's worth the energy.

00:55:04.534 --> 00:55:06.503
Narrator: Gabe Tyler from GT Detailing

00:55:06.503 --> 00:55:09.372
takes us behind the scenes
of reviving this car

00:55:09.372 --> 00:55:10.740
filled with trash.

00:55:10.940 --> 00:55:12.509
The detail started by removing

00:55:12.509 --> 00:55:15.312
the stained seats and floor mats.

00:55:15.312 --> 00:55:17.947
Gabe evaluates whether the
seats need to be removed,

00:55:17.947 --> 00:55:19.916
depending on any stains under the seat

00:55:19.916 --> 00:55:22.052
and between the center console.

00:55:22.052 --> 00:55:23.720
Once the floor was exposed,

00:55:23.720 --> 00:55:27.023
Gabe separated the trash and
customer's personal belongings,

00:55:27.023 --> 00:55:29.025
using his hand to remove the bigger items

00:55:29.025 --> 00:55:31.428
and a vacuum to clean
up any leftover trash.

00:55:32.295 --> 00:55:35.165
He cleaned the door jambs
with a degreaser and brush,

00:55:35.165 --> 00:55:38.668
followed by a power washer
to remove any dirt or gunk.

00:55:38.668 --> 00:55:41.004
This step is done early
in the detailing process

00:55:41.004 --> 00:55:44.607
and before shampooing, in case
water sprays on the carpet.

00:55:44.607 --> 00:55:47.344
A vacuum removed as much
dirt and junk as possible

00:55:47.344 --> 00:55:48.411
from around the car,

00:55:48.411 --> 00:55:50.547
including the floor, seats,

00:55:50.547 --> 00:55:52.248
and under the spare tire cover.

00:55:53.249 --> 00:55:56.586
After vacuuming, Gabe started
the chemical cleaning process,

00:55:56.586 --> 00:55:57.754
working from the top down

00:55:57.754 --> 00:55:58.988
to avoid double cleaning

00:55:58.988 --> 00:56:00.890
due to chemicals and soap dripping down.

00:56:00.890 --> 00:56:03.193
[Spray hisses]

00:56:03.193 --> 00:56:05.095
To remove stains on the headliner,

00:56:05.095 --> 00:56:06.963
he used the spray, drill brush,

00:56:06.963 --> 00:56:08.498
and a microfiber towel.

00:56:10.500 --> 00:56:11.901
He deep-cleaned the floor mats

00:56:11.901 --> 00:56:13.703
with the pressure washer and shampoo,

00:56:13.703 --> 00:56:15.872
and then hung them to dry.

00:56:20.477 --> 00:56:23.213
To restore the seats,
Gabe used a steam cleaner,

00:56:23.213 --> 00:56:26.649
dry brush, and extractor
to remove any stains.

00:56:26.649 --> 00:56:28.485
He prefers to use McCulloch steamers

00:56:28.485 --> 00:56:31.855
because there are multiple
attachments to choose from.

00:56:31.855 --> 00:56:34.224
Moving to the carpet, Gabe
sprayed a carpet cleaner

00:56:34.224 --> 00:56:36.593
and used an agitator to
bring any hidden dirt

00:56:36.593 --> 00:56:41.564
to the surface, sucking it
up with a Mytee extractor.

00:56:41.765 --> 00:56:43.400
He steam cleaned the steering wheel

00:56:43.400 --> 00:56:45.602
and pedals with a brush attachment.

00:56:45.602 --> 00:56:48.071
Steam cleaners help
remelt dried, melted gunk

00:56:48.071 --> 00:56:51.040
like candy and chocolate,
for an easier removal.

00:56:52.175 --> 00:56:55.078
He treated the door panels
with a vacuum, spray,

00:56:55.078 --> 00:56:58.047
steam cleaner, and microfiber towel.

00:56:58.047 --> 00:57:00.984
For plastic surfaces, Gabe
works in small sections

00:57:00.984 --> 00:57:03.653
to ensure he doesn't miss any spots.

00:57:03.653 --> 00:57:05.622
Once the plastic surfaces were clean,

00:57:05.622 --> 00:57:08.124
he applied a plastic UV protector.

00:57:09.225 --> 00:57:11.995
Gabe reinstalled the seats
and floor mats into the car,

00:57:11.995 --> 00:57:13.163
and cleaned the glass,

00:57:13.163 --> 00:57:15.732
And the detailing was complete.

00:57:36.286 --> 00:57:38.388
Narrator: Larry Kosilla
shares the importance

00:57:38.388 --> 00:57:40.056
of keeping your car clean

00:57:40.056 --> 00:57:42.392
and how to prevent
water damage from leaks.

00:57:42.892 --> 00:57:45.762
Hey, I'm Larry Kosilla from AmmoNYC.com.

00:57:45.762 --> 00:57:48.064
We manufacture car-cleaning supplies.

00:57:48.064 --> 00:57:49.933
Today, I'm gonna show you
the step-by-step process

00:57:49.933 --> 00:57:51.935
for cleaning one of the
most disgusting cars

00:57:51.935 --> 00:57:53.369
I've ever worked on.

00:57:53.369 --> 00:57:54.704
Right off the bat, I'm saying

00:57:54.704 --> 00:57:56.339
there's a water leak somewhere.

00:57:56.339 --> 00:57:58.174
There's obviously mice.

00:57:58.174 --> 00:58:00.009
This particular case, it smelled so bad.

00:58:00.009 --> 00:58:02.078
There's urine from mice in there.

00:58:02.078 --> 00:58:03.513
This is kind of the next level,

00:58:03.513 --> 00:58:04.948
where you're cleaning it just to make it

00:58:04.948 --> 00:58:07.150
so that you can safely
operate the vehicle.

00:58:14.557 --> 00:58:16.693
On cases like this, when
there's lots of mold,

00:58:16.693 --> 00:58:17.560
lots of junk,

00:58:17.560 --> 00:58:19.095
I increase the water temperature

00:58:19.095 --> 00:58:21.064
as much as the power washer can handle,

00:58:21.064 --> 00:58:23.500
and that'll increase the cleaning ability.

00:58:23.500 --> 00:58:24.734
When you hit it with water,

00:58:24.734 --> 00:58:27.036
it's gonna indicate where
there might be a leak.

00:58:27.036 --> 00:58:28.638
There's seals, what they call rails

00:58:28.638 --> 00:58:30.206
or rain guards or rain rails.

00:58:30.206 --> 00:58:32.609
If those get packed or compacted with,

00:58:32.609 --> 00:58:34.177
you can see in the door jams,

00:58:34.177 --> 00:58:36.412
muck and gunk and grime and leaves,

00:58:36.412 --> 00:58:38.348
it actually backs up and overflows

00:58:38.348 --> 00:58:40.617
and then flows into the vehicle.

00:58:40.617 --> 00:58:42.952
The debris in the door jams were 100%

00:58:42.952 --> 00:58:45.455
the cause for the water.

00:58:45.455 --> 00:58:47.156
Basically the water pools on the bottom,

00:58:47.156 --> 00:58:48.558
underneath the carpet.

00:58:48.558 --> 00:58:50.393
I use a tiny little brush

00:58:50.393 --> 00:58:51.594
to kinda get in there.

00:58:51.594 --> 00:58:54.497
The idea is to lift a
lot of the door seals

00:58:54.497 --> 00:58:55.932
and get underneath the door seals,

00:58:55.932 --> 00:58:58.468
because if they're not
perfectly flush, again,

00:58:58.468 --> 00:59:00.637
that whole rain issue as the door closes.

00:59:00.637 --> 00:59:02.939
And if there's leaves and junk in there,

00:59:02.939 --> 00:59:04.607
it just won't seal properly.

00:59:12.048 --> 00:59:15.218
So, in terms of buffing
or restoring the paint,

00:59:15.218 --> 00:59:17.687
this mission was to use
a microfiber cutting pad.

00:59:17.687 --> 00:59:19.422
I used a compound.

00:59:19.422 --> 00:59:21.157
And what that microfiber pad does

00:59:21.157 --> 00:59:22.625
is it kind of tickles the surface

00:59:22.625 --> 00:59:23.993
of the paint, so to speak.

00:59:23.993 --> 00:59:25.962
And it removes a lot of the stuff

00:59:25.962 --> 00:59:27.764
that just didn't come out with a wash.

00:59:27.764 --> 00:59:30.099
And if it's embedded in
the pores of the paint,

00:59:30.099 --> 00:59:32.201
just like a blackhead, so to speak,

00:59:32.201 --> 00:59:33.570
I have to go in there and just

00:59:33.570 --> 00:59:35.438
remove all of that, exfoliate it.

00:59:36.372 --> 00:59:38.241
As the vibration, or what we call

00:59:38.241 --> 00:59:39.876
oscillation of the machine, "zzz,"

00:59:39.876 --> 00:59:42.211
as it's buzzing, it's actually vibrating

00:59:42.211 --> 00:59:44.447
a lot of the dirt that's behind

00:59:44.447 --> 00:59:46.516
the headlights and the taillights

00:59:46.516 --> 00:59:47.784
and all these little pieces.

00:59:47.784 --> 00:59:48.985
So, in this case,

00:59:48.985 --> 00:59:51.254
I washed the car for a second time

00:59:51.254 --> 00:59:52.889
after I buffed it,

00:59:52.889 --> 00:59:54.757
then, afterwards, you
wanna protect it again.

00:59:54.757 --> 00:59:56.025
So, in this case, we use a product

00:59:56.025 --> 00:59:57.560
called AMMO Reflex Pro,

00:59:57.560 --> 00:59:58.761
which is a type of coating.

00:59:58.761 --> 01:00:00.530
It's an antimicrobial,

01:00:00.530 --> 01:00:01.798
which is perfect for this car,

01:00:01.798 --> 01:00:02.932
because obviously there's mold

01:00:02.932 --> 01:00:04.767
and bacteria all over the place.

01:00:07.537 --> 01:00:09.005
When it comes to headlights,

01:00:09.005 --> 01:00:11.407
as the sun is penetrating the headlight,

01:00:11.407 --> 01:00:14.077
it's just chewing up all
the UV protection it has.

01:00:14.077 --> 01:00:16.446
And over time, it sort
of just decays, right?

01:00:16.446 --> 01:00:17.981
It becomes dead skin on top.

01:00:17.981 --> 01:00:21.351
So what we need to do is
remove that dead skin.

01:00:21.351 --> 01:00:22.819
Then you can go in just like the paint,

01:00:22.819 --> 01:00:24.621
with a microfiber cutting pad,

01:00:24.621 --> 01:00:27.023
an abrasive, like a compound,

01:00:27.023 --> 01:00:28.424
use your pad, polish it up.

01:00:28.424 --> 01:00:30.426
And you can see within a minute or two,

01:00:30.426 --> 01:00:31.761
it'll rejuvenate that.

01:00:31.761 --> 01:00:33.997
Now you have to put the suntan lotion on.

01:00:33.997 --> 01:00:36.099
And in this case, you
wanna re-clear-coat it.

01:00:37.900 --> 01:00:39.302
With respect to windows,

01:00:39.302 --> 01:00:40.770
the best way that I've
found over the years,

01:00:40.770 --> 01:00:42.505
and this is an evolving process,

01:00:42.505 --> 01:00:44.807
is, first, use a little
bit of window cleaner.

01:00:44.807 --> 01:00:45.642
You spray it down.

01:00:45.642 --> 01:00:47.443
First thing is use a scrub pad.

01:00:47.443 --> 01:00:50.647
That scrub pad is gonna
lift a lot of the oils

01:00:50.647 --> 01:00:53.816
and junk that are stuck
to the glass over time.

01:00:53.816 --> 01:00:54.951
And so as you do that,

01:00:54.951 --> 01:00:56.619
it's still gonna be wet and kinda gross.

01:00:56.619 --> 01:00:59.088
You take what we call towel No. 1,

01:00:59.088 --> 01:01:02.225
and you scoop up all of that oily residue.

01:01:02.225 --> 01:01:04.227
It's not gonna look pretty at this point,

01:01:04.227 --> 01:01:05.194
and that's totally fine.

01:01:05.194 --> 01:01:06.829
So, once it's kind of dry,

01:01:06.829 --> 01:01:07.764
then you go back in,

01:01:07.764 --> 01:01:09.766
lubricate again with a glass cleaner,

01:01:09.766 --> 01:01:10.900
and then use a squeegee,

01:01:10.900 --> 01:01:12.869
working top to bottom for obvious reasons.

01:01:12.869 --> 01:01:14.604
And then you go in with a separate towel

01:01:14.604 --> 01:01:17.040
called No. 2 towel, and
you can wipe it down.

01:01:17.040 --> 01:01:18.841
And sometimes they call them glass towels.

01:01:18.841 --> 01:01:21.411
Then you can really go
in and buff the edges.

01:01:21.411 --> 01:01:24.347
So it's this constant
running after smears.

01:01:27.817 --> 01:01:29.752
When we're vacuuming the inside of a car,

01:01:29.752 --> 01:01:31.120
first thing you wanna look at is the size

01:01:31.120 --> 01:01:33.256
of the debris that you're
trying to vacuum up.

01:01:33.256 --> 01:01:36.359
And then first thing is sort
of vacuum up the big parts.

01:01:42.165 --> 01:01:45.001
Then I put in what's
called a crevice tool.

01:01:45.001 --> 01:01:46.636
A crevice tool sort of
looks like your hand.

01:01:46.636 --> 01:01:47.637
It's very narrow.

01:01:47.637 --> 01:01:49.138
You remove a lot more debris that way,

01:01:49.138 --> 01:01:50.807
because the orifice is much smaller,

01:01:50.807 --> 01:01:54.210
thus the lifting power is much better.

01:01:55.445 --> 01:01:56.946
With respect to shampooing,

01:01:56.946 --> 01:01:58.381
you can use a scrub,

01:01:58.381 --> 01:01:59.949
a red-handle scrub brush,

01:01:59.949 --> 01:02:01.851
they're $2 or whatever.
They're everywhere.

01:02:01.851 --> 01:02:03.186
In this case, we used AMMO Shag,

01:02:03.186 --> 01:02:05.855
which is a cleaner just for fibers,

01:02:05.855 --> 01:02:07.957
carpets, Alcantara,
seats, that kinda thing.

01:02:07.957 --> 01:02:11.127
And then the goal is to
agitate the junk to the surface

01:02:11.127 --> 01:02:13.096
and then wet vac it out.

01:02:13.096 --> 01:02:14.931
I thought I had already sucked up

01:02:14.931 --> 01:02:16.399
a lot of the poo and all that stuff,

01:02:16.399 --> 01:02:17.467
but as I was shampooing,

01:02:17.467 --> 01:02:19.702
you could actually see
little bits of mouse poo

01:02:19.702 --> 01:02:21.537
in the shampoo machine, which is insane.

01:02:21.537 --> 01:02:22.672
That means mouse poo

01:02:22.672 --> 01:02:24.273
was not only on top of the surface,

01:02:24.273 --> 01:02:26.008
but underneath.

01:02:26.008 --> 01:02:28.845
So, when you remove certain
parts of the vehicle,

01:02:28.845 --> 01:02:31.347
like carpets or the rear trunks,

01:02:31.347 --> 01:02:32.715
you can pull that out and shampoo it.

01:02:32.715 --> 01:02:33.616
That's way better,

01:02:33.616 --> 01:02:36.018
because there's just compressed water

01:02:36.018 --> 01:02:37.086
shooting out all the junk.

01:02:37.086 --> 01:02:38.421
It's way better than just sitting there

01:02:38.421 --> 01:02:39.255
and going back and forth.

01:02:39.255 --> 01:02:40.523
But it makes sense, you can't do that to,

01:02:40.523 --> 01:02:42.625
let's say, the carpets
underneath the seats,

01:02:42.625 --> 01:02:44.293
you're not gonna do that.

01:02:44.293 --> 01:02:45.928
OK, so to clean the interior,

01:02:45.928 --> 01:02:48.030
we use a couple of different tools.

01:02:48.030 --> 01:02:49.065
We use AMMO Lather.

01:02:49.065 --> 01:02:51.434
That is designed for
plastic, leather, and vinyl.

01:02:51.434 --> 01:02:53.736
And then we use an interior scrub brush.

01:02:53.736 --> 01:02:55.638
It's really good for buttons and things.

01:02:55.638 --> 01:02:57.006
And then we use a scrub pad.

01:02:57.006 --> 01:02:58.841
Now, the scrub pad basically takes

01:02:58.841 --> 01:03:00.543
that top layer of junk off

01:03:00.543 --> 01:03:02.278
before you come in with
a microfiber towel.

01:03:02.278 --> 01:03:04.347
Then, once you're all
done in your scrubbing,

01:03:04.347 --> 01:03:06.048
'cause what you're doing is
you're agitating the dirt,

01:03:06.048 --> 01:03:08.117
it comes to the surface,
what we call a lather,

01:03:08.117 --> 01:03:11.521
and then you scoop away that junk.

01:03:11.521 --> 01:03:12.688
Once everything is done,

01:03:12.688 --> 01:03:14.791
you take compressed air, blow them out,

01:03:14.791 --> 01:03:16.859
and as the lather sort of squeaks out

01:03:16.859 --> 01:03:18.227
from underneath and the sides,

01:03:18.227 --> 01:03:20.062
just scoop it up with a microfiber towel.

01:03:20.062 --> 01:03:22.398
Let it dry, assess the situation,

01:03:22.398 --> 01:03:24.634
then you can go back in
any spots you've missed.

01:03:26.269 --> 01:03:29.272
The separation between a regular detail

01:03:29.272 --> 01:03:31.474
and an amazing detail is
usually the last, like,

01:03:31.474 --> 01:03:33.509
3, 4, 5%.

01:03:33.509 --> 01:03:35.278
So, you have silver from the car,

01:03:35.278 --> 01:03:37.713
and then you have this
kind of faded, whitish,

01:03:37.713 --> 01:03:39.916
gross material or trim,

01:03:39.916 --> 01:03:41.150
you wanna brighten that up.

01:03:41.150 --> 01:03:44.887
So, I took the pad and applied mud.

01:03:44.887 --> 01:03:47.123
And, again, it's to draw this contrast.

01:03:54.263 --> 01:03:55.798
Even if you're not a car person,

01:03:55.798 --> 01:03:58.034
even if you're not excited
about washing your car,

01:03:58.034 --> 01:03:59.836
there is a level of cleanliness

01:03:59.836 --> 01:04:01.971
that actually translates into dollars,

01:04:01.971 --> 01:04:05.608
into saving your investment in this case.

01:04:05.608 --> 01:04:06.742
You have to keep those

01:04:06.742 --> 01:04:08.411
seals clean at the very minimum,

01:04:08.411 --> 01:04:10.046
especially if you park outside.

